# Protection Money (planned as "The Dark Ages"): an agent-played game of Dynasty. Scenario and steering: scenario.md; deck: seed.md.
# Every say and aside is the reply of the agent playing that seat (see log/yearN/).
# The Storyteller writes the act lines and refs the moves; the engine keeps the books.
# The narrator's lines are not part of the game: they live in narration.md, never here.
# Each chapter opens with a "# ===== CHAPTER N: <title>" marker.
setup([1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 8], "protection-money", "Protection Money",
      "Six players · twelve years · the Emperor pays to keep his throne",
      "Six players and a hard deck: the Emperor, the Official, the Gentry, the Silk and Rice Peasants, and the Warlord. "
      "Rich takes the throne with a set in Year 2, and Clark holds a complete set beside it for ten years, paid thirty a year "
      "to leave it unused. Through three lean years the crown runs its reserve down to nothing and owes nobody, so nobody "
      "calls a revolt; instead it pays towards every peasant's land, and Silke and Paddy grow rich on the sea. Rob takes three "
      "of his four pieces and loses them all to the x4 harvest. In the last year Clark finally spends his set on the throne, "
      "and the pieces it frees carry Paddy and Silke up the table the same night.",
      short="6 players · 12 years")

# ============================== SETUP ==============================
R(0, "Setup", None, "Six players: the Emperor, the Official, the Gentry, the Silk and Rice Peasants and the Warlord (rulebook Section 4 and Appendix A). No Scholar, so the Emperor appoints someone to read I Ching and count the population, and pays the appointee whatever she chooses (Note 3). Each realia province holds two pieces of its own realia, and each collector needs one from each of the other three. The Warlord starts with two soldiers and may hire one more, for 20, after any march (Section 14).")
act("Six booklets: Reina Osorio draws the Emperor, Clark Grafton the Official, Richard Baron the Gentry, Silke Weber the Silk Peasant, Paddy Doyle the Rice Peasant and Robert Land the Warlord.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
deal_grain("Rich, the Gentry, sets out the Harvest Pile and deals 60 to each player. It is the first year's harvest.")
take_stock("Clark, Rich, Silke and Paddy each take two pieces of their own realia out of the box. They wait beside the board until they're sold.")
act("Clark cuts out the Province 2 Irrigation System, one square by two; Rich the Province 3 Reservoir, two by three. They're set aside until built. Nobody sits in Provinces 4 or 7, so nothing is owed there.", map_hl=[2,3])
# Reina's first prompt: who reads I Ching (then the shuffle). Rob's first prompt: his two soldiers (place_army) and his tax stance.
# ===== CHAPTER 1: The Knife Held Up
place_army({"W1":"G11", "W2":"O18"}, "Robert Land stands his two soldiers: W1 on G11, on the strip under the Wall where Clark's Province 2 and Rich's Province 3 meet; W2 on O18, in his desert between Silke's Province 5 and the empty Province 7, two steps from Paddy's Province 6. He declares he'll pay the tax on his own harvest, though the Warlord may refuse.")
say("Rob", "Two lonely soldiers, out in the sand, minding their own business. Purely ornamental, I promise. And yes, Your Majesty, the Warlord pays his taxes like a good citizen. Write that down, Clark. You'll want it on record for later.",
 "Rob: G11 touches both Clark and Rich, and O18 sits right on Silke's doorstep with Paddy one march further on, so whatever they buy at auction, I'm already next door. Paying the tax costs me next to nothing, and it buys me a nice clean reputation for the night I stop being nice.")
say("Reina", "Clark, you keep the tax rolls already, so you'll keep the heavens as well. Shuffle the twenty, read one a year, and tell us honestly when the people start multiplying. And Rob, it's noted: the Warlord pays his taxes. Clark will write it down in his best hand.",
 "Reina: I want the counter with the man I already pay and who already counts things for the crown. If the population is about to double, I want to hear it from a friend first and get my palaces built before the rice bowls run out.")
act("Reina appoints Clark, the Official, to the empty Scholar's job as well: he'll read an I Ching card each year from Year 2 and keep the population counter. What he's paid for it, on top of the Official's salary, is hers to decide at each year's salaries. Clark shuffles the twenty cards and lays them face down.", map_hl=[1,2])

# ============================== YEAR 1 ==============================
R(1, "The first year", None, "No card is drawn. The 60 each player was dealt is the first harvest, and the tax is levied on it.")
CUR_INC.update({s:60 for s in ACTIVE})
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, what a fine choice. Clark will read the heavens beautifully, and Rob paying his taxes is music to us all. Now, Silke, Paddy, my dear friends of the coast: the Harvest Pile is open and the grain will flow to you every harvest, as it should. I ask only the customary five each, the smallest rent in all China. Think of it as a token of friendship.",
 "Rich: Five is the floor, and I'm starting there so nobody minds paying it. I want the habit in place before the population card turns and I come back asking for more. Ten grain doesn't buy me a Dragon Jar, but it makes Silke used to doing business with me, and she's the one sitting on the jars.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays him 5.")
say("Reina", "Half, my friends, while the fields are kind to all of us. Nobody here has a bad year yet, and the crown means to turn this good year into stone. The Summer Palace goes up first, and the Wall and the Canal come after it. Clark, ten for you: five for the rolls and five for the heavens. Rob, I'm looking forward to that tax you promised.",
 "Reina: Everyone's at +60, so this is the cheapest year I'll ever get to tax hard. The Summer Palace unlocks my Wall and my Canal, and I want the reserve working before the population card turns.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 50%: 30 from each province's 60, Rob's included, 150 in all. The crown builds the Summer Palace alone, for 20, and at the year's end pays Clark 10, 5 as the Official and 5 for reading the I Ching.", map_hl=[1])
taxes(.50, skip=(), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Thirty from each of us, my own included, and a hundred and fifty to the crown, every grain counted. Rob, it's in the ledger, in my best hand: the Warlord promised to pay, Year One. And Your Majesty, ten for the rolls and the heavens. I've noted that too, and I'll remember it fondly when the time comes for raises.",
 "Clark: Fifty percent in the first fat year is a bold opening, and I write down every bold opening. Rich's 'token' rent is in the book as well. The fellow who reads the cards and keeps the tally decides what the table remembers, and that kind of leverage is for sale.")
say("Reina", "G24, well back from the sand, where the breeze is cool and the view is unbothered. Rob, the gardens are lovely but the gate is a long walk from you.",
 "Reina: G24 is out of every Warlord soldier's reach this year and next, so Rob can't turn my first palace into a twenty-grain toll booth. It's built now, and that opens the Wall and the Canal in Province 1.")
build("Summer Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Summer Palace", at="G24", text="Public works: Reina builds the Summer Palace on G24, in the east of Province 1. It must stand before any Wall link or the Province 1 canal.", hover="Reina pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. 870 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions: each seller puts up one piece from stock and sets its minimum: Clark's Customs House, Rich's Land Deed Scroll, Silke's Dragon Jar, Paddy's Wooden Bowl. Clark, Rich, Silke, Paddy and Rob each give a limit on every lot at once, and the lots are sold in order; the Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say("Clark", "Customs House, starting at two, and the realm will be better run for it. As for the scrolls, jars and bowls, I'm bidding sensibly. Thirty grain doesn't go far after the crown has taken its half, and yes, I'm noting who took that half.",
 "Clark: Every one of these three pieces is a step toward the set, but it's a ten-year game and I won't starve to buy a bowl in Year One. Small bids, a long memory, and I let the others empty their purses into the Harvest Pile.")
say("Rich", "Fifty percent, Your Majesty, and so wisely spent. The Summer Palace will be the jewel of the north! As for the market, I'll be modest. A little bid here and there, nothing to frighten anyone. My Land Deeds open at three, which is practically giving them away.",
 "Rich: With forty grain, I can't afford to go big on everything, so I've capped each piece at eight. That leaves me enough to pay the taxes and keep the twenty I need for the set. The peasants each have twenty-five after the tax, so Clark's thirty is the only purse I really have to beat.")
say("Silke", "Let me count it out loud, since nobody else will. Reina sits on a hundred and ninety, the Pile on two thousand one hundred and sixty, and the coast has twenty-five apiece after rent and tax. So I won't be driving any prices up. Paddy, if you and I bid each other up for Clark's Customs House, three quarters of it goes into Rich's pile. Let's not make the Gentry rich on our quarrel. And the crown has its palace now. I'd like the next stone to go into land.",
 "Silke: Small, honest ceilings on all three pieces I need, so even if I win everything I still have grain to show for it. A Dragon Jar at three tells them it isn't cheap, and I'm watching Reina's hundred and ninety more than anyone's bid.")
act("The Harvest Pile is the common bank: Rich keeps it and pays the harvests out of it, but its grain isn't his.", map_hl=[3])
say("Paddy", "Twenty-five grain, the lot of it, and I've counted it twice. I'll bid honest and small on what I need, and if someone wants a piece more than I do, it's theirs and no hard feelings. Silke, Clark, Rich, if we each keep it cheap, the Pile gets less of our grain and we all eat. My Bowl goes up at two, fair price for a fair bowl.",
 "Paddy: I need all three pieces and twenty grain on top, so I'm not getting into a fight in Year One. Four a lot at most, and whatever's left goes to the tax and the twenty I'll have to show at the end.")
say("Rob", "Go on, my friends, bid away. Customs houses, deed scrolls, jars, bowls, buy the lot and set them out nice and pretty on your lands. I'm just a simple man of the sand; I'll admire them from a respectful distance.",
 "Rob: Every piece they buy lands on the board and gives my soldiers something to walk toward, and each bidding war sends three quarters of the price into the pile, so they get poorer while I keep my thirty. That thirty pays for a third soldier the moment I want one.")
act("The minimums: Customs House 2, Land Deed Scroll 3, Dragon Jar 3, Wooden Bowl 2. Rob passes on every lot. No lot is close enough at the top for a live raise.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
DC = "Take it, then. I'll have one next year at a better price, and I'll remember the year you outbid me."
DS = "Take it, then. I'll keep my grain and spend it later."
DP = "Go on, it's yours. I'll catch the next one."
lot(2, "CH", [B(6,4), B(6,None,DP), B(5,6), B(5,None,DS), B(3,7)],
 hover="Clark's minimum was 2. Paddy stopped at 4, Silke at 6; Rich's limit was 8. Rich puts the Customs House on K12.", at="K12")
lot(3, "LD", [B(6,4), B(6,None,DP), B(2,5), B(2,None,DC), B(5,6)],
 hover="Rich's minimum was 3. Paddy stopped at 4, Clark at 5; Silke's limit was 6. Silke puts the Land Deed Scroll on P25.", at="P25")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(6,4), B(6,None,DP), B(2,5), B(2,None,DC), B(3,6)],
 hover="Silke's minimum was 3. Paddy stopped at 4, Clark at 5; Rich's limit was 8. Rich puts the Dragon Jar on K13.", at="K13")
lot(6, "WB", [B(2,5), B(2,None,DC), B(5,5), B(5,None,DS), B(3,6)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 2. Clark and Silke both stopped at 5; Rich's limit was 8. Rich puts the Wooden Bowl on K14.", at="K14")
act("Rich holds a Customs House, a Dragon Jar and a Wooden Bowl, the three pieces his set needs, and 22.5 grain, over the 20 a set requires: a complete set. A player with a complete set may change places with another at any moment (Section 11), and the Gentry may take any seat, the throne included. He holds it for now.", map_hl=[3], big=True)
say("Rich", "Oh, look at that, a little collection! Purely decorative, Your Majesty, like Rob's soldiers. I'll set them well back from the sand where nobody can trip over them. And my loyalty to the throne is exactly as solid as it was five minutes ago, which is to say, entirely.",
 "Rich: A complete set in Year One. I'm not using it today, because the knife is worth more held up than swung. I've parked all three pieces deep in the south of my province, away from Rob's reach. I'll take a seat when the throne wobbles or someone gets above themselves, and everyone at this table knows it now.")
say("Silke", "P25, as far down the coast as my province goes, well clear of Rob's ornamental friends. And look at the table, everyone. Rich bought three lots in one evening and is sitting on a complete set with grain to spare. Your Majesty, I'd count your throne cushions tonight. The Gentry can sit wherever he likes now, and I don't think the Harvest Pile is what he has in mind.",
 "Silke: My deed sits in the far corner where no soldier can reach it for two years, and I'm down to about twenty grain. Rich is the danger now, so I want Reina worrying about him rather than about squeezing the coast.")
march({"W1":"H10", "W2":"P19"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps off the Wall strip onto H10, in Rich's Province 3, and W2 onto P19, in Silke's Province 5. Each raids an empty square: 5 from Rich and 5 from Silke.",
 hover="Rich drops from 22.5 to 17.5 grain, below the 20 a set needs, so he can't use his set until he has 20 again.")
say("Rob", "Rich, old friend, I've just remembered you once promised me a tour of your lovely little collection. I'm on my way now, and I'll take five at the door for the trouble. Silke, you're on the way too, so five from you as well. Purely a courtesy call.",
 "Rob: Taking five off Rich leaves him on 17.5 grain, under the 20 a set needs, so his ticket to the throne is gone the moment my boot hits H10, and Reina will think I did it for her. W1 is three steps from those three pieces at K12 to K14, and W2 is walking down the coast toward Silke's scroll.")
salaries({2:10}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark 10, as she declared: 5 as the Official and 5 for reading the I Ching.")

# ============================== YEAR 2 ==============================
R(2, "Not a cloud in months", "blight")
act("Clark turns the first I Ching card: Drought. Every province collects an average harvest, and the population holds at x1, 100 million.", map_hl=[2])
say("Clark", "The heavens speak: 'Drought. Not a cloud in months. The locusts, at least, are having a fine year. All provinces collect an average harvest.' The population stands at one hundred million, multiple one, so read the x1 row. No personal disasters this year, and the only creatures doing better than the Gentry are the locusts. Rich, I've written your three lots down too: Customs House at seven, Jar at six, Bowl at six, and a set you can't use until Rob gives you back that five.",
 "Clark: I lost three lots by a single grain apiece, and that's in the ledger, not forgiven. The useful part is that Rich now has a set and Rob just pushed him back under twenty. The Emperor needs someone who keeps count of exactly that, and that someone expects to be paid more than ten.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
act("With the harvest Rich has 52.5 grain, so his Customs House, Dragon Jar and Wooden Bowl are a complete set again: he may change places with anyone at any moment, the throne included.", map_hl=[3], big=True)
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum again, and each pays him 5.")
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, I've adored every moment of your reign, truly I have, all one and a half years of it. But Rob has shown us all how quickly a pretty collection can be taken, and I'd hate to waste such a fine set. So I'll take the cushions Silke mentioned, with the deepest respect, and Reina, the Harvest Pile is yours, and it's in excellent order. Silke, Paddy, the usual five, and then you'll have a new Emperor who remembers his friends.",
 "Rich: Rob raided me once and took me below twenty, and the tax is still to come, so a set I hold is a set I can lose. The chair came cheap, and I'm taking it while the price is right.")
swap(3, 1, "Rich, holding a full set and 62.5 grain, changes seats with Reina and takes the throne. He takes the crown's whole reserve, 195, for 257.5 in all. Becoming Emperor returns all his pieces to their provinces' stock: the Customs House to Province 2, the Dragon Jar to Province 5, the Wooden Bowl to Province 6. Reina moves to Province 3 as the Gentry, holding nothing, and draws 60 from the Harvest Pile.",
 hover="Reina's appointment of Clark to read the I Ching stands unless the new Emperor changes it; his pay for it is now Rich's to set.")
say("Rich", "My friends, my dear, dear friends, the new reign begins with mercy: thirty percent, not fifty. Remember who lowered it. Clark keeps the rolls and the heavens at the same pay as before, and the crown digs a canal so the realm can eat. Reina, the Pile is in good hands with you. Rob, I'm counting on that tax you promised in Year One, and Clark has it in writing.",
 "Rich: A light tax buys me goodwill and keeps everyone's grain low enough that nobody finishes a set in a hurry. The canal adds twenty a year to my own harvest, and since every piece I'd need to worry about is back in someone else's stock, I want the reserve growing faster than theirs.")
act("Rich declares a tax of 30%: 10.5 each from Clark, Reina, Silke and Paddy, and 3 from Rob's +10, 45 in all. The crown digs both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 1, for 80, and at the year's end pays Clark 10, 5 as the Official and 5 for reading the I Ching.", map_hl=[1])
taxes(.30, skip=(), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Thirty percent, Your new Majesty, and so recorded: ten and a half each from me, Reina, Silke and Paddy, and three from Rob, forty-five to the crown. Rob, you paid, and that's twice now in my book. Reina, you'll want to know that I wrote down the hour you lost the throne, and who it was that warned you.",
 "Clark: One coronation in two years, and the man who did it holds a 257-grain treasury while I hold the pen. Rich lowered the tax to buy goodwill, and Reina has reason to want the throne back. I can serve either of them, for a price.")
build("Grand Canal", 80, {1:80}, canal={1:2}, text="Public works: Rich digs both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 1, at the crown's cost of 80. Each adds 10 to the Emperor's harvest from next year.", hover="Rich pays the 80, into the Harvest Pile. The Summer Palace, which the Province 1 canal needs first, already stands. 790 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions: Clark puts up a Customs House, Reina a Land Deed Scroll (Province 3's stock, now hers to sell), Silke a Dragon Jar and Paddy a Wooden Bowl, and each sets the minimum. Clark, Reina, Silke, Paddy and Rob each give a limit on every lot at once; the Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say("Clark", "My Customs House opens at two again. Last year I lost three lots by a single grain apiece, and I have the receipts. This year the Official has a full purse and a long memory, so anyone planning to bid me up should know I've already written their name down.",
 "Clark: There's only one Land Deed left in Reina's stock, and Paddy needs it as badly as I do, so that's the one I pay up for. The jar and bowl get honest bids, because if I win all three I still keep the twenty grain a set needs, and then I have the leverage that unseated Reina in my own hands.")
say("Reina", "Well played, Rich. I mean it. One and a half years, and the jewel of the north is yours to enjoy. I'll keep the Pile honest and pay every harvest to the grain. And I'm bidding, everyone. I'd rather not, but I'm not going to pretend I don't want my chair back. Silke, you warned me and I didn't listen. I won't make that mistake twice.",
 TH(("Reina", "Nine a lot leaves me twenty-two and a half if I win all three. That's enough to hold a set and walk straight back into the palace I built. I'll pay a grain more to keep Clark from getting two pieces this round, because he's the one quietly writing down everybody's weak hour."),
    ("Reina, whispering to Silke", "You saw it coming before any of us. If I'm back on the throne, the coast gets the first Canal in Province 5 and a light tax while incomes are thin. Let me have a clear run at the Dragon Jar and the Bowl this round.")))
say("Silke", "Here's the count, since Clark only writes it down. His Majesty has two hundred and twenty-two and a half, and a canal that waters only his own province. Down on the coast I have thirty-five, after Rob's courtesy call, Rich's 'usual five' and the crown's ten and a half. Paddy, Reina, I'm bidding plainly for a Customs House and a Bowl. Anyone who pushes those prices up is just filling the Pile.",
 "Silke: If I take both at my ceilings I'm left with exactly twenty grain, a whole set, and then Reina's Gentry seat is within reach. A peasant who doesn't climb when the ladder's there gets stepped on.")
say("Paddy", "Well, Your new Majesty, thirty's a kinder number than fifty, and I'll say thank you for it plainly. I've forty-six grain and I'm bidding a bit firmer this year, I'll tell you that straight. Silke, you're right about the Pile: you take the first Customs House cheap and leave me a fair go at the Jar, and neither of us feeds Reina's pile on a quarrel.",
 "Paddy: Last year I went too gentle and Rich got the lot, so this year I'll pay up to about eight or nine a piece, and that still leaves me the twenty a set needs. The Land Deed is the one to watch, there's only one left and Clark's got the grain to chase it.")
say("Rob", "Your Majesty Rich, congratulations. I always said you had the bottom for that chair. Reina, my condolences, and welcome to Province 3. My man at H10 will be a very attentive neighbour. Buy whatever you like, all of you. I'm only here to watch.",
 "Rob: That's two of them now who know a set means the throne, so whatever lands on the board this year is a crown in pieces, and I've got a soldier in Reina's front garden. Let them overpay for it, then I'll go and collect.")
act("The minimums: Customs House 2, Land Deed Scroll 3, Dragon Jar 3, Wooden Bowl 2. Rob passes on every lot. On the Dragon Jar and the Wooden Bowl, Reina's limit of 10 is one step over Clark's 9, so each of them gets one live raise on both.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say("Clark", "Reina, I like you, I truly do, but I've watched one set put an Emperor in the Pile this year already, and I won't watch a second one get built across the table from me. Sixteen apiece on the Jar and the Bowl. Rich, Your Majesty, you can thank me later, and the thanks should come in grain.",
 "Clark: If I take both at sixteen, I'm left with twenty-six grain and a Land Deed, Jar and Bowl, which is a full set of my own, and the tie goes to my seat. Even if I only take one, Reina's set is broken, and the man on the throne owes me a good deal more than ten a year.")
say("Reina", "Clark, write this down in your best hand: I'm not stopping at ten. Silke, Paddy, you'll each get your quarter, and I'm sorry it comes out of my purse and not out of the Gentry's quarrel with the crown.",
 "Reina: Eleven and a half twice leaves me exactly 20, the bare minimum to hold the set. If I win both, I swap before Rob's man at H10 can take five off me, because I'm not giving him the chance Rich got.")
DC = "Yours, then, at a price I'd call vanity. It's noted, with the date."
DE = "Take it, then. I'll be at the Pile, counting."
DS = "Yours. I'm not making the Pile any fatter for it."
DP = "Fair enough, it's yours. I won't spend grain I can't spare."
lot(2, "CH", [B(5,8), B(5,None,DS), B(6,8), B(6,None,DP), B(3,9)],
 hover="Clark's minimum was 2. Silke and Paddy stopped at 8; Reina's limit was 9. Reina puts the Customs House on M14.", at="M14")
lot(3, "LD", [B(6,9), B(6,None,DP), B(2,10)],
 hover="Reina's minimum was 3. Paddy stopped at 9; Clark's limit was 12, and 14 to keep it from Paddy. Clark puts the Land Deed Scroll on I15.", at="I15")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(6,8), B(6,None,DP), B(3,11.5), B(3,None,DE), B(2,12.5)],
 hover="Silke's minimum was 3. Paddy stopped at 8. After the live raise Reina stood at 11.5 and Clark at 16. Clark puts the Dragon Jar on I16.", at="I16")
lot(6, "WB", [B(5,7), B(5,None,DS), B(3,11.5), B(3,None,DE), B(2,12.5)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 2. Silke stopped at 7. After the live raise Reina stood at 11.5 and Clark at 16. Clark puts the Wooden Bowl on J16.", at="J16")
act("Clark holds a Land Deed Scroll, a Dragon Jar and a Wooden Bowl, the Official's three pieces, and 33 grain: a complete set. He may change places with anyone at any moment, the throne included. He holds it for now.", map_hl=[2], big=True)
say("Clark", "A Scroll, a Jar and a Bowl, set well back from the sand, and thirty-three grain beside them. Your Majesty, the throne is quite safe for now. I'm a patient man and a reasonable one, and a reasonable Official is paid like one. Ten a year was the price for a man without a set. Shall we talk about the new rate?",
 TH(("Clark", "Everyone gets to see a loaded set on the table and nobody knows when I'll use it. Rich has a 222-grain treasury he'd sooner keep, Reina wants her chair back, and both of them now have to bid for my patience, so I'll hold the set and collect."),
    ("Clark, whispering to Rich", "Your Majesty, a set I don't use is worth a good deal to you. Thirty a year in salary, starting this year, and I stay the loyal Official keeping your rolls. Miss a payment and I'll have the date written down before the I Ching card is turned.")))
say("Reina", "M14, deep in the south of my new province, and Rob's attentive neighbour will need a long walk to admire it. Clark, that was very well done. Rich, you'd better count your cushions tonight. I know exactly how that goes.",
 "Reina: One piece down and two to go, and it's tucked well away from the soldier at H10. Clark now holds a full set and the Emperor has the deepest purse at the table, so I'm going to make sure those two keep looking at each other and not at me.")
march({"W1":"I11", "W2":"P20"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto I11, in Reina's Province 3, and W2 onto P20, in Silke's Province 5. Each raids an empty square: 5 from Reina and 5 from Silke.")
hire_soldier("M15", "Rob hires a third soldier, W3, for 20 to the Harvest Pile, and stands it on M15, the desert notch beside Reina's Customs House on M14. His army is now at its limit of three. W3 moves from next year.")
say("Rob", "Reina, I hear M14 is lovely this time of year, so I've sent a fellow to stand just across the road at M15 and admire it. Five from you and five from Silke for the hospitality. Clark, Your Majesty, I'm not after anyone's throne. I'm just a man who likes to see the sights.",
 "Rob: The new man at M15 is one step from Reina's Customs House and three from Clark's Bowl, W1 is heading east for Clark's Scroll, and W2 is walking down to Silke's. Now I've got three soldiers and three targets, and every one of them knows it.")
say("Rich", "Clark, my most diligent servant, the realm has never had rolls so beautifully kept. Twenty-five for the Official and five for the heavens. A man of your quality is paid like one, and I'm proud to say so in front of everyone. Rob, enjoy the sights, though I'd remind you that M15 is a very long walk from my palace.",
 TH(("Rich", "Thirty a year is a rounding error next to the two hundred and twenty I'd hand Clark if he jumped into my chair tonight, so I'm buying him and paying in full. The catch is that every grain I give him keeps his set alive, so next year I'd like Rob or the auctions to thin him out."),
    ("Rich, whispering to Clark", "Thirty, paid tonight, and I've done it publicly so the table sees you're my man. Here's my word, exactly as I'll keep it: thirty a year, every year, for as long as you hold the Official's chair and I hold this one. Not one grain short and not one day late.")))
salaries({2:30}, text="Salaries: Rich pays Clark 30, 25 as the Official and 5 for reading the I Ching, three times what he declared.")

# ===== CHAPTER 2: Not One Grain Short

# ============================== YEAR 3 ==============================
R(3, "Fat years make large families", "double2")
act("Clark turns the year's I Ching card: Population Doubles. Every province collects a good harvest, and the population doubles from x1 to x2, 200 million: every province eats twice what it did. From now on any province that may develop new land can buy it, 20 a square.", map_hl=[2])
say("Clark", "\"Population Doubles.\" Fat years make large families, friends. Every province collects a good harvest, and the counter goes from one hundred to two hundred million: that's the x2 row on your income tables, everybody, x2. Read it properly, because I'll be taxing it properly. And Rich, more mouths means more work for the man who counts them. I'll just mention that now, so it's on the record when we settle up at year's end.",
 "Clark: A good harvest fills every purse I tax, but doubled mouths mean my bare strip under the Wall now loses more than ever, so the thirty from Rich matters twice as much. Twice the people also means twice the grumbling, and every grumble is another line in my book.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Reina", "The Pile pays out to the grain: twenty each to Clark, Silke, Paddy and myself, nothing to the crown or the desert, and anyone's welcome to count it after me. Rent is the floor, five each, and not a grain more. Silke, Paddy, think of it as a deposit, because I'll be paying it back several times over when I come asking about that Jar and that Bowl you're both still holding.",
 "Reina: Two years ago the throne was mine and Rich's set took it, so this year the Pile stays honest, the rent stays at the minimum, and the two pieces I still need are sitting beside the board with the coast. If I keep the peasants sweet, one good auction gets me the set, with Clark and his thirty a year watching from the other side.")
rents(5, "Rents: Reina, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays her 5. It's her first rent as Gentry.")
say("Rich", "Thirty percent again, friends. Doubled families deserve an undoubled tax, and you may tell your grandchildren the Baron reign never squeezed a cradle. The crown pays half of every new field in China, and I'm raising a Winter Palace so the canals can reach Silke and Paddy next. Clark, your thirty, as promised: not one grain short and not one day late. I heard you mention 'more mouths', and I heard that thirty is what I promised.",
 "Rich: The Winter Palace is cheap, and it opens the way to the coastal canals, which buys me two grateful peasants against any move on this chair. Half a subsidy on every square keeps me safe from revolt without paying for Clark's whole farm, and those two squares of my own should stop the crown losing money every year.")
act("Rich declares a tax of 30%: 6 each from the four provinces with +20; the crown and Rob had no income this year, so 24 in all. The crown builds the Winter Palace alone, for 20; it must stand before the canals in Provinces 5 and 6. New land: the crown pays half, 10 of every 20, of any square Silke or Paddy buys this year, and buys two sea squares for Province 1 itself. At the year's end it pays Clark 30.", map_hl=[1])
taxes(.30, skip=(), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Thirty percent, six apiece: six from Reina, six from Silke, six from Paddy, and six from my own purse, so nobody can say the collector skips himself. That's twenty-four to the crown, counted twice. Rob, your zero is in the book next to your promise, and I'll be reading both again the year you've got income. Rich, I heard you, word for word. It's the same words I've already got written down.",
 "Clark: Rich quoting my own promise back at me tells me he knows I'm keeping score, and that's the point of the set: it's worth more loaded than fired. Thirty a year is honoured for now, and Reina just started bidding for the peasants' realia, so I'm watching who builds a second set.")
say("Rich", "The Winter Palace goes on H20, right in the heart of Province 1, where the Emperor can winter in comfort and the canal surveyors can set out for the coast. And two new fields out of the sea at F23 and F24, so the crown feeds itself and stops eating into the reserve that pays all of you.",
 "Rich: H20 is out of every soldier's reach this year and next, so Rob gets nothing from my palaces but a long walk. The two sea squares turn my minus twenty-five a year into roughly level, and a crown that isn't bleeding grain is much harder to topple.")
build("Winter Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Winter Palace", at="H20", text="Public works: Rich builds the Winter Palace on H20, in the middle of Province 1. It must stand before the canals in Provinces 5 and 6.", hover="Rich pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile.")
buy_land(1, ["F23", "F24"], {1:40}, "New land: Rich buys two sea squares for Province 1, F23 and F24, for 40 from the crown. Each adds 20 to the Emperor's harvest from next year.")
say("Silke", "Three squares on the coast, Rich: ten from me and ten from you on each one, just like you said, and I'm counting it out loud so nobody misremembers. That's thirty from my purse and thirty from the crown's two hundred and sixteen and a half. Your Winter Palace can wait for its canal, because this field will be paying sixty a year before the plaster's dry.",
 "Silke: I'm down to twelve grain tonight, but my harvest goes from minus five to plus fifty-five, and if the next card is x4 I won't be the one in debt. I took the far squares out by the water because Rob's soldiers have to walk past Paddy's rice to reach them.")
buy_land(5, ["N26", "N27", "O27"], {5:30, 1:30}, "New land: Silke buys three sea squares for Province 5, N26, N27 and O27, paying 30 herself; the crown pays the other 30, half of each. They add 60 to her harvest from next year.")
say("Paddy", "Two fields down at the far southern corner, please, Rich: ten of my grain and ten of the crown's for each, like you offered. I'll thank you properly when they're green. And Rob, they're a long way from where your lads are camped, so let's keep it that way.",
 "Paddy: The numbers on my card say I'm losing five a year as I stand, and they'll get worse if the population card comes again, so two fields at half price set that right from next year. I've still got thirty-eight left, which keeps my twenty back for a set. I picked the corner as far from Rob's soldier on P20 as I could get.")
buy_land(6, ["S26", "T26"], {6:20, 1:20}, "New land: Paddy buys two squares for Province 6, S26 and T26, at the sea's edge in the far south, paying 20 himself; the crown pays the other 20. They add 40 to his harvest from next year.")
act("The auctions: Clark puts up a Customs House, Silke a Dragon Jar and Paddy a Wooden Bowl, and each sets the minimum. Province 3's stock is gone. Clark, Reina, Silke, Paddy and Rob each give a limit on every lot at once; the Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[2,5,6])
say("Clark", "My Customs House goes up at two, and the buyer gets the best paperwork in the north with it. As for the Jar and the Bowl, I've already got mine, so I'm only watching. Mostly watching. Reina, you told everyone you'd be calling on Silke and Paddy, so you can hardly blame me for showing up too.",
 "Clark: Reina is a Jar and a Bowl away from a loaded set of her own, and a second gun at this table halves what mine is worth to Rich. I'll pay to block her on either lot, but never so much that I fall under twenty and unload my own.")
say("Reina", "The Customs House is the coast's business, so I'll keep my hands off it, and I'd ask the coast to do me the same courtesy with the Jar and the Bowl. Every grain we throw at each other here goes three parts in four into the Pile, and I'm the one who has to count it back out to you.",
 "Reina: I've got sixty-two, the set needs twenty in hand, so I can spend forty-two between the two pieces and not a grain more. Silke can't go past twelve on the Bowl, so the real fight is Paddy for the Jar, and I'm hoping the Customs House eats his purse first.")
say("Silke", "I've got twelve grain, everyone, and I'll say it before anyone else counts it for me. Three fields went into the sea this afternoon, so I'll bid honest and small. Paddy, you and I both need that Customs House, and if we drive each other up, three quarters of it goes into the Pile and the only winner is Clark's purse. My Jar opens at four, because it's a good Jar and I won't give it away.",
 "Silke: Next year my harvest pays fifty-five, so I'd rather lose a Customs House tonight than go into debt chasing one. If Paddy takes it, I'll get the next one cheaper from a table that's already spent.")
say("Paddy", "I'm in for Clark's Customs House this time, properly. Six lots I've let go by a grain, and I'd like to bring one home. The Jar I'll bid on honestly, but Reina's purse is fatter than mine, so if it goes north, no hard feelings. My Bowl starts at four. It's a good bowl, I ate out of it for years.",
 "Paddy: Silke's only got twelve, so sixteen should finally win me the Customs House unless someone bids just to block me. I'm keeping the Jar bid modest because new fields come before a jar, and Clark's Customs House is the piece I can't get anywhere else.")
say("Rob", "Pass, pass, and pass, friends. I've no pottery to finish and no customs to collect, so I'll leave the bowls and jars to the honest folk. And Paddy, my lads are camping exactly where they're camping. Nobody's ever been hurt by a tent. Clark, put a little star by my name in that book of yours: Rob kept his grain out of everyone's way.",
 "Rob: Every grain Reina and Silke throw at each other over that Bowl, three quarters of it goes into the Pile, and I keep my thirty-seven for the day a soldier has to move fast. W3 is one step from Reina's Customs House, and the less they spend watching me, the better.")
act("The minimums: Customs House 2, Dragon Jar 4, Wooden Bowl 4. Rob passes on every lot. No lot is close enough at the top for a live raise.", map_hl=[2,5,6])
DS = "Take it, then. I'll be reading the price you paid out loud next year."
DP = "That's past my purse. It's yours, and well bought."
DE = "Then it's yours, and I'll remember who made me pay for it."
lot(2, "CH", [B(5,8), B(5,None,DS), B(6,9)],
 hover="Clark's minimum was 2. Silke stopped at 8; Paddy's limit was 16. Paddy puts the Customs House on S24.", at="S24")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(6,12), B(6,None,DP), B(3,28), B(3,None,DE), B(2,29)],
 hover="Silke's minimum was 4. Paddy stopped at 12, Reina at 28. Clark didn't need a Jar; his limit, only to keep it from Reina, was 35. Clark puts the Dragon Jar on H14.", at="H14")
lot(6, "WB", [B(5,4), B(5,None,DS), B(3,14), B(3,None,DE), B(2,15)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 4. Silke stopped at 4, Reina at 14. Clark didn't need a Bowl; his limit, only to keep it from Reina, was 20. Clark puts the Wooden Bowl on G15.", at="G15")
act("Every Dragon Jar and Wooden Bowl at the table is now on the board: Clark holds two of each, and every piece in stock is sold. Reina, who needs a Jar and a Bowl, can't finish a set unless someone sells or loses one.", map_hl=[2,3], big=True)
say("Paddy", "Seven lots it took me, but I've finally bought something! Thank you, Clark, it'll sit down at S24 well away from anyone's tents. Silke, you bid fair and small like you said you would, and I won't forget it. And Clark, you've got two Jars and two Bowls now, so I hope you're planning to sell some of those on to the rest of us.",
 "Paddy: The Customs House goes deep in the south at S24, which my card says no soldier can reach for two rounds. But look at Clark: he's bought pieces his set doesn't even need, just so nobody else can finish theirs, and I'll be keeping an eye on him from here on.")
say("Clark", "Reina, you asked the coast to stay out of your way. You never asked me, and I don't remember promising. The Jar goes on H14 and the Bowl on G15, well back from the tents Rob says never hurt anyone. Paddy, congratulations on the Customs House, and that's one more line in the book with your name on it, a good one this time.",
 "Clark: That's forty-four grain for pottery I don't need, but now Reina can't finish a set, and if Rob's soldiers ever reach I16 or J16 I've got a second Jar and Bowl in reserve. I'm still above twenty, so my set stays loaded and I'm the only one at the table with one.")
march({"W1":"H12", "W2":"Q21", "W3":"M14"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto H12, in Clark's Province 2, raiding 5 from Clark; W2 onto Q21, in Paddy's Province 6, raiding 5 from Paddy, his first raid; and W3 onto M14, where it takes Reina's Customs House and carries it into the desert on N14. It's the first piece Rob has taken.",
 hover="Province 6 has now been raided, so any soldier Paddy buys may move. Rob's objective: one piece from each of Clark, Reina, Silke and Paddy; Reina's is the first.", take={"W3":("CH","N14")})
say("Rob", "Reina, my dear, I'm only borrowing your Customs House, and it'll be perfectly safe out on N14 with a soldier keeping an eye on it. Clark, five from your fields for the walk, and you can write it in your book in your nicest hand. And Paddy, I did say tents never hurt anyone. I never said they don't move.",
 "Rob: That's the first piece in the bag, and my objective's started at last. W1 is two steps from Clark's new Jar and W2 is three from Paddy's shiny Customs House, so they can all spend next year's grain building walls instead of buying thrones.")
salaries({2:30}, text="Salaries: Rich pays Clark 30, as Official and for reading the I Ching, as he promised.")

# ============================== YEAR 4 ==============================
R(4, "Heaven has favourites", "heaven")
act("Clark turns the year's I Ching card: Heaven Has Favourites. Clark's Province 2 and Silke's Province 5 collect a good harvest; Rich, Reina, Paddy and Rob a bad one. The population holds at x2, 200 million.", map_hl=[2])
say("Clark", "\"Heaven Has Favourites.\" Good harvests in Provinces 2, 4 and 5: that's me and Silke, and Province 4 is empty, so nobody gets that one. Bad harvests in 1, 3, 6, 7 and 8: Rich, Reina, Paddy and the desert. Population stays at two hundred million, x2, same row as last year. I only read the card, friends, I didn't write it. Rob, I've written down your five in my nicest hand, and also where your soldier is standing, two squares from my Jar.",
 "Clark: The card's kind to me, but a lean year for the crown is a test of Rich's thirty, and Reina and Paddy are now both poorer and both raided. Rob's man on H12 is the real worry, since my spare Jar is in range, and that's exactly why I bought a spare.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; the negative incomes are paid into it.")
say("Reina", "The Pile pays what the card says and takes what the card says: eighty to Silke, twenty to Clark, ten to Paddy, and thirty out of my own purse, which I'm counting into it in front of you. Paddy, a bad year gets the floor, five. Silke, heaven smiled on you, so I'm asking ten, and I'd call that a neighbour's price rather than a landlord's.",
 "Reina: Rob walked off with my Customs House, Clark has bought every Jar and Bowl at the table, and after this harvest I'm down to two grain, so I'm starting over from the bottom. Silke took eighty this year and can spare ten, and keeping Paddy on five means the coast has no reason to think about revolt.")
act("Reina asks Paddy for the minimum rent of 5 and Silke for 10. Above the minimum, rent is a negotiation: Silke owes 5 because she was asked, and may agree to more. (Reina has 32 grain after the harvest.)", map_hl=[3,5,6])
say("Silke", "Ten it is, Reina, and I'll count it out in front of everyone: five because you asked, and five because you said yourself the Customs House is the coast's business. If you say it again now, out loud, the ten is yours. And Rich, two more squares on the coast at half from the crown, same as last year. The province that's eating well this year shouldn't be the one asking for canals next year.",
 "Silke: Five grain is cheap if it buys me one rival fewer for the next Customs House, now that Rob's left Reina needing one too. The two new fields cover me if the x4 card turns up, and Clark's sitting on two Jars and two Bowls he doesn't need, so the Bowl I'm after is going to cost me.")
say("Reina", "Five it is, Silke, and no hard feelings. When I said that, I had a Customs House on M14, and now it's sitting in Rob's tent on N14, so I'm not going to promise away the one thing I need for five grain. Here's what I will say in front of everyone: the coast and I aren't going to bid each other into the Pile, and if you and I both want the same piece, we'll talk it over before the auction.",
 "Reina: Five grain isn't worth a public vow to sit out the next Customs House, not now Rob has mine and I've got nothing left to start a set with. Silke's sharp, and I'd rather she respected me for refusing than caught me breaking my word a year from now.")
rents(5, "Rents: Silke and Paddy each pay Reina the minimum, 5.")
say("Rich", "Thirty percent, the same as last year and the year before. A crown that changes its word with the weather isn't worth bowing to. Silke, half of M26 and M27, as asked and as offered. Clark, your thirty, to the letter. And I'll raise a little Marble Pagoda, a modest one, so heaven sees that someone in Province 1 still has manners after a bad harvest.",
 "Rich: The reserve is down to sixty-six and Province 1 just had a lean year, so I'm building the cheapest thing on the list and buying no land of my own until the harvest refills the coffers. Clark's thirty hurts more every year, but a broken promise to him would cost a great deal more than thirty.")
act("Rich declares a tax of 30%: 6 from Clark, 24 from Silke and 3 from Paddy; Reina and Rob had negative incomes and owe nothing. 33 in all. The crown builds the Marble Pagoda alone, for 20, as the year's only project. New land: the crown pays half, 10 of every 20, of any square Silke or Paddy buys this year, and buys none for Province 1. At the year's end it pays Clark 30.", map_hl=[1])
taxes(.30, skip=(), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Thirty percent, counted out loud like everything else this year: six from me, twenty-four from Silke, three from Paddy, nothing from Reina or Rob on red ink. That's thirty-three to the crown. Silke, you're carrying the empire this year, and I've written that down too, because one day somebody will forget it. Reina, I've also noted that \"the coast's business\" lasted exactly one year.",
 "Clark: The crown's living off Silke's luck now, and it spends twenty here, twenty there on pagodas while my thirty keeps coming. I'll keep a running tally of the reserve, because the year it runs thin is the year Rich's promise gets tested.")
say("Rich", "The Pagoda goes on G20, a quiet little spot between the palaces where the monks can pray for better harvests for all of us. Rob, it's nowhere near your tents, so I trust it'll stay a place of peace.",
 "Rich: G20 is out of every soldier's reach this year and next, so Rob's lads can't touch any piece I've built yet. I'm still buying no new land: after the Pagoda, Silke's half and Clark's thirty, the crown has about thirty left, and that's thin enough already.")
build("Marble Pagoda", 20, {1:20}, key="Marble Pagoda", at="G20", text="Public works: Rich builds the Marble Pagoda on G20, between the two palaces in Province 1. Nothing else is built this year.", hover="Rich pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile.")
buy_land(5, ["M26", "M27"], {5:20, 1:20}, "New land: Silke buys two more sea squares for Province 5, M26 and M27, paying 20 herself; the crown pays the other 20. They add 40 to her harvest from next year.")
say("Paddy", "Just the one field this year, Rich, at half like you offered, down at U27 with my back to the sea. It was a bad harvest and Rob's had his first five off me, so I'm not going to pretend I'm flush. Rob, if your tent's going to move, I'd take it kindly if it moved away from me.",
 "Paddy: One square keeps me ahead in case the population doubles again, and I've still got nineteen and a half in my purse, near enough the twenty I'll need. I'm putting everything as far south as it'll go, because that soldier on Q21 is three rounds from my Customs House.")
buy_land(6, ["U27"], {6:10, 1:10}, "New land: Paddy buys one more square for Province 6, U27, in the far south-east, paying 10; the crown pays 10. It adds 20 to his harvest from next year.")
act("No auctions this year: every piece of starting stock has been sold, and nobody has sold one back to the pile.", map_hl=[])
march({"W1":"H13", "W2":"R22", "W3":"L15"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto H13, in Clark's Province 2, one square from Clark's Dragon Jar on H14; W2 onto R22, in Paddy's Province 6, two from his Customs House on S24; W3 onto L15, in Reina's Province 3. Each raids an empty square: 5 each from Clark, Paddy and Reina.")
say("Rob", "Paddy, you asked my tent to move, and look, it's moved! Only a little bit, and in your direction, but nobody can say I didn't listen. Clark, write down five more in that lovely hand of yours, and Reina, five from you for the road. Bad harvests make everyone a bit snappish, so I'm only taking the small change.",
 "Rob: W1 is one step off Clark's Jar on H14, W2 is two off Paddy's Customs House, and W3 is heading for Clark's Bowl through Reina's garden. Next year three provinces will be wondering which one of them I really mean, and the answer is all three.")
salaries({2:30}, text="Salaries: Rich pays Clark 30, as Official and for reading the I Ching, as he promised. The crown's reserve is down to 19.5.")

# ===== CHAPTER 3: Owing Nobody

# ============================== YEAR 5 ==============================
R(5, "The seed rotted", "dribble1")
act("Clark turns the year's I Ching card: Bad Harvest. Every province collects a bad harvest. The population holds at x2, 200 million.", map_hl=[2])
say("Clark", "\"Bad Harvest. The seed rotted, the rains came late, and the ox went lame. All provinces collect a bad harvest.\" Population holds at two hundred million, times two, so read the x2 row, bad-harvest column, everyone. And Rich, a bad year with nineteen and a half in the treasury. I've started a new page for that.",
 "Clark: A lean harvest everywhere means less tax for me to collect and less grain for Rich to keep his thirty-a-year promise with, and this is exactly the squeeze I wrote about in my diary. My set's loaded and I've got ninety-nine grain, so this year I find out what his word is really worth.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; the negative incomes are paid into it.")
act("Storyteller's tip, to Paddy, said to the whole table: the rulebook lists seven causes for revolt: the Emperor is unjust; taxes are consistently excessive; the reserve is too low; the works have been delayed too long; the Emperor can't control the Warlord; the Emperor won't subsidise new land; or any player is in debt. A revolt needs its caller and two others agreeing; the Emperor may answer it, and it stands only if all three still back it. If it stands, the next Emperor is acclaimed by counting grain in hand, soldiers on the board and friends.", map_hl=[6])
say("Reina", "Pile's paid out to the grain: seventy to Silke, thirty to Paddy, and the north pays in, me included. Rent's five each, the floor, same as every year. Silke, you came out of a bad harvest seventy up, and I'm seven down to the bone, so if you'd like to send ten instead of five I'll take it with thanks. No conditions, and I'm not asking for anything back. And Clark, while you're writing new pages, put down that the Gentry hasn't squeezed the coast once.",
 "Reina: Seven grain, no pottery, no Customs House. Being in debt is a revolt clause, and I won't be the one who gives Clark that excuse. So I take the floor, keep my name clean with the coast, and wait for the Official to make his move on a treasury holding nine and a half.")
act("Reina asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum rent of 5 each, and asks Silke, with no conditions, whether she'll pay 10. Above the minimum, rent is a negotiation: Silke owes 5 and may agree to more. (Reina has 7 grain after the harvest; the crown has 9.5.)", map_hl=[3,5,6])
say("Silke", "Ten, Reina, and since nobody else at this table will count it, I will. Last year I offered you ten for a promise and you took five, and this year you're asking ten for nothing. I'll pay it anyway, because a Gentry sitting on seven grain is one bad card from debt, and I'd rather a neighbour who can pay her way than one who'll end up begging the crown. Clark, put that on your new page next to Rich's nine and a half: the coast is holding up the north again.",
 "Silke: Ten grain buys me nothing on paper, but if this table ever counts friends to pick an Emperor, I want the Gentry to remember who fed her in the bad year. That leaves me a hundred and ten, more than everyone else at the table together, so I'd better have friends before somebody starts calling it hoarding.")
rents(5, "Rents: Silke and Paddy each pay Reina the minimum, 5.")
act("Silke pays Reina 5 more, freely: 10 in all.", deltas={pid(5):-5, pid(3):5}, map_hl=[3,5], deal=True)
say("Rich", "Thirty percent, my friends, the same as last year and the year before. I said a crown that changes its word with the weather isn't worth bowing to, and a bad harvest is exactly that kind of weather. Clark, your thirty is set aside first, not one grain short, and you can write that on your new page in ink. Silke, Paddy, the crown still pays toward every field you put into the sea. It's a smaller share this year, but it's paid. No new works this year, and I won't pretend otherwise. The Great Wall waits one season so the throne doesn't have to borrow from the people it protects.",
 "Rich: Thirty from the coast puts me at thirty-nine and a half, Clark's promise takes thirty, and a few grain of goodwill toward the coast leaves me just about breathing. I'm not touching the rate, because Silke is counting every grain out loud and one more cause for revolt is the last thing I need on her list.")
act("Rich declares a tax of 30%: 21 from Silke and 9 from Paddy; Clark, Reina and Rob had negative incomes and owe nothing. 30 in all. No public work this year. New land: the crown pays 5 of the 20 on each province's first new square this year, and buys none for Province 1. At the year's end it pays Clark 30.", map_hl=[1])
taxes(.30, skip=(), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Twenty-one from Silke, nine from Paddy, nothing from the north or the desert, and that's thirty for the crown, every grain counted. Rich, I've written down your \"not one grain short\" in ink like you asked, and right under it that the crown has nine and a half after the Pile, and that the Wall waits a season. Reina, your clean record with the coast is on the page too. I'm a fair clerk.",
 "Clark: Do the arithmetic: thirty in, thirty out to me, so the reserve finishes the year at nine and a half, with the works delayed again. That makes two of the seven causes for revolt sitting in my book right now.")
say("Silke", "Two more fields, Rich. Fifteen of mine and your five on the first, then ten and ten on the second. That's fifteen from a crown that just took twenty-one off me, so you're getting most of my tax back as a sea wall. Every one of these squares pays forty to the Pile in a good year, and none of them ever asks for a palace. Clark, write that the coast's buying land while the throne's buying time.",
 "Silke: That puts seven fields by the sea and still leaves me sixty-four, plenty for a Bowl or a Customs House when one comes up. I went north up the coast toward L instead of south, because Rob's soldier on R22 is only a few steps from anything I'd put on P or Q.")
say("Paddy", "One field, Rich, out at U28 by the water where Rob's tent at R22 has the furthest to walk. I'll be straight with you: at fifteen of mine I'd drop under the twenty I told this table I'd keep back, so I'm asking ten and ten, same as you gave Silke on her second. If that's too much for the crown this year, say so and I'll keep my grain and won't hold it against anyone.",
 "Paddy: Ten and ten leaves me twenty and a half, so I keep my word on the twenty and still get a field that pays twenty every year from next harvest. I put it in the far corner because that soldier at R22 is one step from my land and I'm not feeding him.")
act("Silke asks for two squares, L26 and L27: L26 at 15 hers and the crown's offered 5, L27 at 10 and 10. Paddy asks for one, U28, at 10 and 10. Both ask the crown for more than it offered, so Rich must answer. (The crown has 39.5, and owes Clark 30 at the year's end.)", map_hl=[1,5,6])
say("Rich", "My friends, I'll give you the crown's arithmetic straight, because Clark is writing it down anyway. Thirty-nine and a half in the treasury, thirty of it already Clark's, to the letter, and that leaves nine and a half for the whole coast. Silke, the five on L26 stands as offered, and on L27 I can put in half a grain, no more. Paddy, I can't do ten. Four is everything I have left, and if it doesn't suit, keep your grain and I'll think none the worse of you. The crown ends this year at zero, but it ends it owing nobody.",
 "Rich: Every grain I've got is spoken for: Clark's thirty, five for Silke, four for Paddy and half a grain for the look of it, and the treasury lands on exactly nothing. If Paddy keeps his grain I'm four to the good. If he doesn't, at least nobody can say this Emperor refused to subsidise new land.")
buy_land(5, ["L26"], {5:15, 1:5}, "New land: Silke buys L26 for Province 5, paying 15; the crown pays 5. It adds 20 to her harvest from next year.")
act("Rich offers half a grain toward Silke's L27, so 19.5 would be hers, and 4 toward Paddy's U28, so 16 would be his. Each peasant decides.", map_hl=[1,5,6])
say("Silke", "I'll take it at nineteen and a half, Rich, and thank you for the half grain. Clark, write it down exactly: half a grain from the crown and nineteen and a half from the coast, on a field that pays twenty a year from next harvest whether the throne can afford it or not. And Rich, five on L26, a half here and four to Paddy comes to every grain you had left after Clark's thirty, so this year the crown can't say it held anything back from us.",
 "Silke: One harvest pays for a field, and even after this one I keep fifty-four and a half, more than Rich, Reina and Rob put together, with the twenty for the set still safe. If Rich empties the treasury and the works stay unbuilt another year, the table has a reason for revolt, and I'm the one with the grain to count.")
buy_land(5, ["L27"], {5:19.5, 1:0.5}, "New land: Silke buys L27 for Province 5, paying 19.5; the crown pays 0.5. She now has seven sea squares.")
say("Paddy", "That's fair, Rich, and it's honest, and I said I'd keep my grain if it didn't suit, so I'll keep it. I'd be fourteen and a half again, and I told this table twenty. U28 will still be there next year when the crown's had a harvest, and I'll ask you again then.",
 "Paddy: It's hard to turn down a field that pays for itself, but I promised twenty and the coast doesn't need a Rice Peasant who says one thing and does another. If that population card comes I'll be sorry, but I'd rather be short a field than short my word.")
act("Paddy buys no land this year. No auctions: there's no stock left, and nobody has sold a piece back to the pile.", map_hl=[6])
march({"W1":"H14", "W2":"S23", "W3":"L16"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto H14 in Clark's Province 2 and takes Clark's spare Dragon Jar out to M15 on the desert; W2 onto S23 in Paddy's Province 6, one square from his Customs House on S24, raiding 5 from Paddy; W3 onto L16 in Reina's Province 3, raiding 5 from Reina. Clark still holds a Jar on I16, so his set is complete.",
 hover="Rob's objective: one piece from each of Clark, Reina, Silke and Paddy. He now has Reina's and Clark's.", take={"W1":("DJ","M15")})
say("Rob", "Clark, old friend, I'm only borrowing the Jar. You've got two of them, and a man who writes everything down can surely spare one for a fellow who pays his taxes. Paddy, five for the walk, and I promise I'll wipe my boots. Reina, sorry, love, it's just the road. You know I'd never mean it.",
 "Rob: That's Clark's piece in the bag, so the clerk with ninety-odd grain and the full set has a hole in it now, and he'll be in a mood to listen when the chair comes up. W2 is one step from Paddy's Customs House, and W3 is strolling off toward Rich's palaces, because an empty treasury only needs one more little push.")
salaries({2:30}, text="Salaries: Rich pays Clark 30, as Official and for reading the I Ching, as he promised. The crown's reserve is down to 4.")

# ============================== YEAR 6 ==============================
R(6, "A new levy", "utility")
act("Clark turns the year's I Ching card: Utility Tax. Every province collects an average harvest, and every province pays the Emperor 2, with 2 more from each province with a public work. Only Province 1 has works, so the other five pay 2 each. The population holds at x2, 200 million.", map_hl=[2])
say("Clark", "\"Utility Tax. A new levy, for the upkeep of things you may or may not have. All provinces collect an average harvest. Every province pays the Emperor a two million utility tax. Every province with a Public Works pays two million more.\" Population holds at two hundred million, times two, so read the x2 row, average harvest. Only the Emperor's province has works, so the rest of us, Rob included, pay the crown two each after the harvest, and that's ten for a treasury that's sitting on four. Rob, the Jar's in the book as \"borrowed,\" and so is the smile.",
 "Clark: Rich kept his word to the grain, but it cost him the whole treasury, so the crown ended the year at four and a lucky card is all that's propping it up now. I'm still loaded with ninety-nine and a full set, and Rob just showed me that my spare was only ever there to be taken.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; the negative incomes are paid into it.")
act("The utility levy: Clark, Reina, Silke, Paddy and Rob each pay the crown 2, 10 in all.", deltas={pid(2):-2, pid(3):-2, pid(5):-2, pid(6):-2, pid(8):-2, pid(1):10}, map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Reina", "Pile's paid out: a hundred and thirty-five to Silke, fifty-five to Paddy, fifteen to the crown, and the north and the desert pay in. Rent's the floor this year, five each and no more. Silke, you were right to count it out loud. You gave me ten when I had nothing to give back, and the way I'll repay it is by not asking twice. And Rich, a crown that ends the year on four, with the Official holding a full set and a Jar walking into the desert, ought to be counting more carefully than I am.",
 "Reina: If there's a revolt, the next Emperor is chosen on grain, soldiers and friends. I've no grain and no soldiers, so friends are all I have, and the coast is the only place I can make them. Taking the floor from a woman sitting on a hundred and eighty-seven is cheap, and she won't forget it.")
rents(5, "Rents: Silke and Paddy each pay Reina the minimum, 5.")
say("Rich", "Thirty percent again, my friends. A good harvest doesn't change the crown's word any more than a bad one did. Paddy, you asked me fair and waited without a word of complaint, so U28 is ten and ten this year, as promised. The spade goes into your coast as well: the first section of the Grand Canal in Province 6, paid for in full by the crown. Clark, your thirty as always, and you can write down that the works have started again.",
 "Rich: Fifty-seven in tax, the canal and Clark's thirty, and the crown still keeps a small cushion. The canal and the ten-and-ten go to Paddy because a revolt needs three people, and the quiet man who keeps his word is the one I most want to keep out of the count.")
act("Rich declares a tax of 30%: 40.5 from Silke and 16.5 from Paddy; Clark, Reina and Rob had negative incomes and owe nothing. 57 in all. The crown digs one section of the Grand Canal in Province 6, Paddy's, for 40. New land: the crown pays 10 of Paddy's U28 and 5 of one more square from any other province. At the year's end it pays Clark 30.", map_hl=[1,6])
taxes(.30, skip=(), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Forty and a half from Silke, sixteen and a half from Paddy, and that's fifty-seven for the crown, every grain counted. Rich, I've written that the works have started again, and right underneath it the sum: twenty-nine plus fifty-seven is eighty-six, then forty for the Canal, fifteen for the coast and my thirty, which leaves the crown one grain to see it through the year.",
 "Clark: He's paying for one cause for revolt with another: the works start moving again, but the reserve drops from four to one. Reina's started counting out loud too, and when two of us are keeping the same book, the third and fourth names come easy.")
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={6:1}, text="Public works: Rich digs the first section of the Grand Canal in Province 6, Paddy's, at the crown's cost of 40. It adds 10 to Paddy's harvest from next year.", hover="Rich pays the 40, into the Harvest Pile. The Winter Palace, which the canals in Provinces 5 and 6 need first, already stands. 710 of the works are still owed.")
say("Silke", "Three fields, Rich, up the coast at K25, K26 and K27. I'll take your five on the first, and on the other two I'm asking half a grain each, the same as last year, so the crown's last grain does some work too. And I'll say it while Clark's pen is out: the first section of the Grand Canal went to Paddy's province, and I don't begrudge him it, but I paid forty and a half of this year's fifty-seven, so the second section had better have salt water in it.",
 "Silke: Fifty-four grain makes it ten fields by the sea next year, and I'm still holding eighty-eight, enough for the set's twenty and a Bowl and a Customs House the moment either one comes up for sale. Rob's walking Jars around the north for now, and every field I put up at K is another step he'd have to march to reach me.")
buy_land(5, ["K25"], {5:15, 1:5}, "New land: Silke buys K25 for Province 5, paying 15; the crown pays 5, as offered.")
say("Paddy", "Ten and ten on U28, Rich, just like you said, and thank you, and for the Canal too. A crown that remembers what it told a rice farmer a year ago is worth bowing to. I'll take the one field and leave the crown its one grain.",
 "Paddy: That leaves me forty-seven, well over my twenty, with a field and a canal section both paying from next year, so if that population card turns up I'm covered. The rest of the purse is for a Jar and a Land Deed, and Clark's got a full set now, so I'd better start asking soon.")
buy_land(6, ["U28"], {6:10, 1:10}, "New land: Paddy buys U28 for Province 6, paying 10; the crown pays 10, as offered. He now has four squares.")
act("Silke also asks for K26 and K27 at 19.5 hers and half a grain from the crown on each, one grain more than Rich offered. Rich must answer. (The crown has 31, and owes Clark 30 at the year's end.)", map_hl=[1,5])
say("Rich", "Half a grain each, Silke, and gladly. You've found the one use for the crown's last grain that nobody at this table can argue with. Clark, write it down: thirty-one in, one to the coast, thirty to you, and the crown ends the year at zero with every grain it owes paid. As for the next spadeful, Silke, I heard you, and I don't forget who pays the most.",
 "Rich: That's the treasury emptied to the grain again, but Silke's six new fields are six more fields I can tax at thirty percent next year. I said I heard her about the canal, and I didn't say I'd dig it.")
buy_land(5, ["K26", "K27"], {5:39, 1:1}, "New land: Silke buys K26 and K27 for Province 5, paying 19.5 each; the crown pays half a grain on each. She now has ten sea squares.")
act("No auctions: there's no stock, and nobody has sold a piece back.", map_hl=[])
march({"W1":"I15", "W2":"S24", "W3":"L17"}, "Rob marches: W2 steps onto S24 in Paddy's Province 6 and takes Paddy's Customs House out to T23 on the desert; W1 steps onto I15 in Clark's Province 2, onto Clark's Land Deed Scroll, and takes 5 instead of the piece; W3 onto L17 in Reina's Province 3, raiding 5 from Reina. A raided square is never raided again, so Clark's Scroll on I15 is now safe where it stands.",
 hover="Rob's objective: one piece from each of Clark, Reina, Silke and Paddy. He now has Reina's, Clark's and Paddy's; only Silke's is left.", take={"W2":("CH","T23")})
say("Rob", "Paddy, I'm so sorry, the Customs House just looked lonely without the Canal next to it. I'll keep it dusted. Clark, look, I'm standing right on your Scroll and I'm only taking the five, so put that in the book under 'restraint.' And Reina, it's only the road again, love. Rich, lovely Canal, by the way.",
 "Rob: That's Paddy's piece, so only Silke's Scroll is left, and W2 is three steps from it. W1 is sitting on Clark's Scroll as a friendly reminder that I could break his set whenever I like, and W3 is wandering toward the Winter Palace, where every raid costs a crown on zero another twenty.")
salaries({2:30}, text="Salaries: Rich pays Clark 30, as Official and for reading the I Ching, as he promised. The crown ends the year with nothing.")

# ===== CHAPTER 4: A Grievance with a Number on It

# ============================== YEAR 7 ==============================
R(7, "The Wall is breached", "breach")
act("Clark turns the year's I Ching card: The Wall Is Breached. Every province collects a good harvest. No Wall link stands above Provinces 1, 2 or 3, so Rich, Clark and Reina each pay 2 in damages to the Harvest Pile instead of 5 for repairs; Silke, Paddy and Rob each play the first personal disaster in their booklets (Province 4 is empty). The population holds at x2, 200 million.", map_hl=[2])
say("Clark", "\"The Wall Is Breached.\" Good harvest for every province, population holds at times two, so the counter reads 200 million and everyone reads the x2 row. Then the horsemen. Provinces 1, 2 and 3 have no wall above them, so Rich, Reina and I each pay 2 to the Harvest Pile after the harvest. Silke, Paddy and Rob, play the first personal disaster in your booklets. Province 4 is empty, so nobody pays there. For the record, Rich: seven years on the throne and not one link of wall over the north. I'll pay my two. I'll also write it down.",
 "Clark: Of all the cards, it's the one that proves the crown never built the Wall, read out by me to a treasury holding zero, with my set loaded and a hundred and fifteen grain after the repairs. That's three causes for revolt now, and I'm watching Silke's face while she reads her disaster.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; the negative incomes are paid into it.")
act("The Wall is breached: no link stands above Provinces 1, 2 or 3, so Rich, Clark and Reina each pay 2 in damages to the Harvest Pile.", deltas={pid(1):-2, pid(2):-2, pid(3):-2}, pile_delta=6, map_hl=[1,2,3])
disaster(5, "a storm off the coast wrecks the boat the Silk Peasant's sons take out to the new fields, and a new one must be built.")
disaster(6, "the Rice Peasant's only water buffalo breaks its leg, and the planting stops while a new one is found.")
disaster(8, "the Warlord is wounded in the legs in a skirmish with the horsemen through the breach. No raids on Provinces 5, 6 or 7 this year.")
say("Reina", "The Pile pays out a good year: Silke two hundred and twenty, Paddy one hundred and ten, Rich forty, Clark and I twenty each, Rob nothing, and the three of us under the broken Wall put our two back in. Paddy, it's the floor, five. Silke, I'm asking ten. It's the first good harvest the coast has had since I took this job, and I've taken the floor from you in every bad one. No conditions, and I won't ask for more than that this year.",
 "Reina: Thirty-three grain and nothing on the board is no position to take anything back from, so I'm asking for ten from the one woman at this table with three hundred, and only in the year she can most easily spare it. Clark's just written down seven years with no Wall in the north, and once he moves I mean to have grain in hand and the coast still on my side.")
act("Reina asks Paddy for the minimum rent of 5, and Silke, with no conditions, for 10. Above the minimum, rent is a negotiation: Silke owes 5 and may agree to more. (Reina has 33 grain after the harvest; Silke has 304.)", map_hl=[3,5,6])
rents(5, "Rents: Silke and Paddy each pay Reina the minimum, 5.")
say("Silke", "Ten, then, Reina, and I'll count it out so nobody says I paid quietly: two hundred and twenty came in, four went to the sea, ten to you, and the crown's hand hasn't even reached my purse yet. You asked honestly with no conditions, so you get it. Rich, Clark, the coast has paid its rent and paid for the Wall you didn't build, so when the tax comes round I expect it to buy something with salt water in it.",
 "Silke: Five grain is nothing next to two hundred and ninety, and a Gentry with forty-three in her purse remembers who paid her in full. Still, I've just read out that she's one of the seats I can take once I own a Customs House and a Bowl, so I'd rather she owes me than resents me.")
act("Silke pays Reina 5 more, freely: 10 in all. (The breach damages were paid by Rich, Clark and Reina; Silke's 4 was her own disaster.)", deltas={pid(5):-5, pid(3):5}, map_hl=[3,5], deal=True)
say("Rich", "Thirty percent, my friends, the same as last year and the year before. The crown's word doesn't blow away with the horsemen. Silke, you'll get your salt water: the first section of the coast canal is dug in Province 5 this year, at the crown's whole cost, because you asked for it and you paid for it. Clark, your thirty, not one grain short, and write down the Wall as well. I'm keeping a little grain in the treasury this year so that when I start on it, it'll be with real money and not just promises.",
 "Rich: One canal section keeps Silke's purse friendly and her tongue busy counting, and one square in F21 starts my own hedge against that x4 card. And for the first time in three years the crown ends the year with something in the jar, sixty-odd grain if the coast doesn't go on a buying spree.")
act("Rich declares a tax of 30%: 66 from Silke, 33 from Paddy and 6 each from Clark and Reina; Rob's income is 0. 111 in all. The crown digs the first section of the Grand Canal in Province 5, Silke's, for 40. New land: the crown pays 5 of every square another province buys this year, and buys a square of sea for Province 1 itself. At the year's end it pays Clark 30.", map_hl=[1,5])
taxes(.30, skip=(), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Thirty percent, collected and counted out loud: Silke sixty-six, Paddy thirty-three, Reina six, my own six, and Rob nothing, which he says he pays, and I've written down that he says so. That's one hundred and eleven for the crown, every grain of it accounted for. Rich, I've entered the canal in Province 5 and the grain you're keeping back, and I've written \"the Wall\" on a fresh line. I'll leave that line empty until you give me something to put on it.",
 "Clark: For the first time in four years the crown has a reserve, about a hundred and nine before my thirty, so Rich has bought himself some breathing room and Silke her salt water. That's two of my friends looked after in one stroke. My set's still loaded, but I'll keep my hand off it until the Wall line either fills or stays empty.")
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={5:1}, text="Public works: Rich digs the first section of the Grand Canal in Province 5, Silke's, at the crown's cost of 40. It adds 10 to Silke's harvest from next year.", hover="Rich pays the 40, into the Harvest Pile. The Winter Palace, which the canals in Provinces 5 and 6 need first, already stands. 670 of the works are still owed.")
say("Silke", "Six more fields, Rich, at your five a square: that's ninety from me and thirty from the crown, and they all go into the Pile in plain sight. And thank you for the canal, honestly. That's the first stone the crown has laid on my coast in seven years, and I'll say so in front of Clark's book. But the Wall line is still empty, and every grain of salt water I'm given goes back out in tax the next year, so dig the Wall before the horsemen take a second bite.",
 "Silke: Each field pays for itself in a year, so ninety grain now comes back as a hundred and twenty a year from next year, and I still keep a hundred and thirty-eight for the day Clark sells a Bowl or Rob gets tired of guarding a Customs House. Sixteen fields and a canal: whoever ends up on that throne will have to talk to the coast.")
buy_land(5, ["J25", "J26", "J27", "K28", "L28", "M28"], {5:90, 1:30}, "New land: Silke buys six squares of sea for Province 5, J25, J26, J27, K28, L28 and M28, paying 15 each; the crown pays 5 each, as offered. She now has sixteen sea squares.")
say("Paddy", "Three more fields, Rich, at your five a square: forty-five from me, fifteen from the crown, all into the Pile where Clark can see it. I've lost a buffalo this year and I won't pretend it didn't sting, but a hundred and ten from one harvest is more than I ever thought I'd see. I'm keeping well over my twenty back, and Rob, that's partly because I'd like to talk to you about a Customs House at T23 before the year's out.",
 "Paddy: I've put the new fields down at the far end, out by U28, and away from that soldier sitting on S24, because Rob's legs will heal by next year. That leaves me seventy grain, enough to pay Rob fairly for my piece back and still have a Jar and a Deed to save for.")
buy_land(6, ["U29", "V28", "V29"], {6:45, 1:15}, "New land: Paddy buys three squares for Province 6, U29, V28 and V29, paying 15 each; the crown pays 5 each, as offered. He now has seven squares.")
act("Rich asked to buy F21 for Province 1, but F21 is already one of Province 1's own squares. Province 1's new land is the sea touching the province or its bought squares (F23, F24); the strip below the Wall opens only once the four links above it stand.", map_hl=[1])
say("Rich", "Forgive an old man for not knowing his own map, Storyteller. One square, then, E24, in the sea beside the fields I already have, twenty into the Pile where Clark can watch it go in. That still leaves the crown Clark's thirty and a little over, and nobody can say this Emperor buys land with money he owes.",
 "Rich: Silke bought six and Paddy three. I can buy one, and the most important thing is that it doesn't put me back at zero: after Clark's thirty I'll have fourteen in the jar. It's a small field, but the x4 card counts fields, and next to the Wall it's about as far from Rob's soldiers as my land can get.")
buy_land(1, ["E24"], {1:20}, "New land: Rich buys E24 in the sea for Province 1, the crown paying the 20. Province 1 now has three sea squares.")
act("No auctions: there's no stock, and nobody has sold a piece back.", map_hl=[])
march({"W1":"H16", "W2":"R25", "W3":"K18"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto H16 in Clark's Province 2, raiding 5 from Clark; W3 onto K18 in Reina's Province 3, raiding 5 from Reina; W2 wades out of Paddy's province onto R25 in the sea, raiding nothing (his wound bars raids on Provinces 5, 6 and 7 this year).",
 hover="Rob's objective: one piece from each of Clark, Reina, Silke and Paddy. He has Reina's, Clark's and Paddy's; only Silke's is left.")
say("Rob", "My legs are bandaged, my friends, so the coast is safe from me this year. W2 is only going for a paddle. Clark, five more for my medical bills, and Reina, five from you as well. I'd call it a get-well card. Paddy, I'm always happy to talk about T23, but that Customs House has become very dear to me, so bring a big purse and a kind word.",
 "Rob: W2 wades out to R25, two steps from Silke's Scroll, the last piece I need, and nobody can stop me walking there while I'm 'wounded.' Meanwhile W1 and W3 are drifting south toward the Winter Palace and the Pagoda, and Rich has only forty-four in the treasury and owes Clark thirty. A raid there costs the crown twenty, so I'm just getting into position.")
salaries({2:30}, text="Salaries: Rich pays Clark 30, as Official and for reading the I Ching, as he promised. The crown ends the year with 14, its first reserve in three years.")

# ============================== YEAR 8 ==============================
R(8, "A careless year", "careless")
act("Clark turns the year's I Ching card: Careless Prosperity. Every province collects a good harvest, and every player plays their next personal disaster: the first for Rich, Clark and Reina, the second for Silke, Paddy and Rob. The population holds at x2, 200 million.", map_hl=[2])
say("Clark", "\"Careless Prosperity. Good times make careless people.\" Every province collects a good harvest, and the population holds at times two, so the counter reads 200 million and everyone reads the x2 row. Then every one of us plays a personal disaster. Rich, Reina and I play our first, and Silke, Paddy and Rob their second. I'll note that the card means all of us, and not just the man who spent the Wall money on a square of sea.",
 "Clark: Rich has paid my thirty for a sixth year, and I've written that down honestly. The crown finished on fourteen again, though, with the Wall line still empty and Rob's man on my doorstep. Careless prosperity is the right card for this treasury, and with a hundred and fifty-four in my purse I can afford to watch who gets careless first.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; the negative incomes are paid into it.")
disaster(1, "a rare horse, sent up the Silk Road as tribute, throws the Emperor in his excitement to ride it.")
disaster(2, "on an inspection of the garrisons, the Official's chariot drops a wheel and runs into a tree.")
disaster(3, "the family's head chef retires after forty years, and the Gentry gives him the send-off he's owed.")
disaster(5, "a fire in the drying sheds takes a season's silk, and the looms stand idle until it's replaced.")
disaster(6, "the river floods the Rice Peasant's lowest paddies, and the seed has to be bought twice.")
disaster(8, "one of the Warlord's most trusted lieutenants runs off with his daughter to start a band of raiders. No raids at all this year: he is too busy feasting the newlyweds.")
say("Reina", "Second good year in a row, so the Pile pays it out: Silke three hundred and fifty, Paddy one hundred and seventy, Rich sixty, Clark and I twenty each, Rob nothing. Silke, ten again, the same as last year and on the same terms, which is no terms. Paddy, I've taken the floor from you every year I've held this job, bad years included. This year you're carrying two hundred and thirty, so I'm asking ten. If you'd rather pay five, say so and I'll leave it there.",
 "Reina: Fifty-two grain is still well short of anything I could buy a set with, and no raids this year means whatever the coast pays me stays in my purse. I'm asking politely, I'm offering Paddy a way out in front of everyone, and I'm letting the crown and Clark watch which of us the coast pays without being made to.")
act("Reina asks Silke and Paddy each for 10 rent, with no conditions. Above the minimum, rent is a negotiation: each owes 5 and may agree to more. (Reina has 52 grain after the harvest; Silke has 478, Paddy 230.)", map_hl=[3,5,6])
rents(5, "Rents: Silke and Paddy each pay Reina the minimum, 5.")
say("Silke", "Ten again, Reina, same terms, which is no terms, and I'll count it: three hundred and fifty in, ten to the fire, ten to you, and four hundred and sixty-three left for Rich to take his thirty percent of. Rob, enjoy your paddle. W2 is two rounds from my Scroll, and I'd rather your lieutenant kept running.",
 "Silke: Rob's soldier is parked two rounds from the one piece I own, so this year's quiet is borrowed, and next year I want the Gentry and the crown both looking at my coast when he comes. With four hundred and sixty-three in the purse I can outbid anyone at this table for a Bowl or a Customs House, so I'm watching whether Rob prices T23 for Paddy or for me.")
act("Silke pays Reina 5 more, freely: 10 in all. (She has 468 after it, and the tax is levied on her harvest income, +350, not on her purse.)", deltas={pid(5):-5, pid(3):5}, map_hl=[3,5], deal=True)
say("Paddy", "Ten it is, Reina, and gladly. You took five off me in the lean years and never squeezed, so in a fat year you'll have ten without me haggling over it. The river took ten off me too, mind, so I'll count it out: a hundred and seventy came in, ten went to the flood, ten to you, and the rest waits for Clark.",
 "Paddy: Five grain is cheap for staying on good terms with the woman who runs the Harvest Pile, and I don't like being the one who paid less than he could afford. I'm saving the big purse for Rob and that Customs House, like he told me to.")
act("Paddy pays Reina 5 more, freely: 10 in all.", deltas={pid(6):-5, pid(3):5}, map_hl=[3,6], deal=True)
say("Rich", "Thirty percent, my friends, the same as it's been for seven years. And Clark, take out your pen, because you've got something to write on that empty line: two links of the Great Wall above Province 1 go up this year, eighty grain, all of it from the crown. Silke, Paddy, five a square again on anything you buy. Clark, your thirty, not one grain short.",
 "Rich: Two links of the Wall won't stop a horseman yet, but they do fill Clark's empty line before he can turn it into a charge sheet. After his thirty, the coast's fields and one more square of sea for me, the crown should still end the year with forty-odd, which counts as a fortune by my recent standards.")
act("Rich declares a tax of 30%: 105 from Silke, 51 from Paddy and 6 each from Clark and Reina; Rob's income is 0. 168 in all. The crown builds two links of the Great Wall above Province 1, for 80. New land: the crown pays 5 of every square another province buys this year, and buys F25 for Province 1. At the year's end it pays Clark 30.", map_hl=[1])
taxes(.30, skip=(), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Thirty percent, counted out loud: Silke a hundred and five, Paddy fifty-one, Reina six, my own six, and Rob nothing, said and noted. That's a hundred and sixty-eight for the crown, every grain of it accounted for. Rich, the pen's out and the line isn't empty any more: two links of the Wall, over Province 1. I've written the province number in as well, so nobody misreads whose roof got mended first.",
 "Clark: Fair's fair, the Wall line finally has ink on it, and I'll say so. But both links went over the Emperor's own fields, and my strip stays shut until all four links above Province 2 stand. That's a grievance with a number on it now, and those are the ones that hold up when it counts.")
build("Great Wall", 80, {1:80}, wall={1:2}, text="Public works: Rich builds two links of the Great Wall above Province 1, his own province, at the crown's cost of 80.", hover="Rich pays the 80, into the Harvest Pile. The Summer Palace, which the Wall needs first, already stands. Two more links above Province 1 open its strip for new land. 590 of the works are still owed.")
buy_land(1, ["F25"], {1:20}, "New land: Rich buys F25 in the sea for Province 1, the crown paying the 20. Province 1 now has four sea squares.")
say("Silke", "Eight more fields at your five a square, Rich: a hundred and twenty from me, forty from the crown, all of it into the Pile. And Clark, write it down properly: two links of Wall, both over Province 1, paid for out of a hundred and five of my grain. I'm glad the north has a roof, but I'll remember whose harvest paid for it. I'm buying west and north, away from the beach where W2 is paddling.",
 "Silke: That makes twenty-four fields, which is four hundred and eighty a year at the x2 row once they're all in. I've kept them out of R25's reach and I still hold two hundred and forty-three, enough to buy a Bowl and a Customs House outright the moment either one comes up for sale.")
buy_land(5, ["I25", "I26", "I27", "I28", "J28", "K29", "N28", "O28"], {5:120, 1:40}, "New land: Silke buys eight squares of sea for Province 5, I25, I26, I27, I28, J28, K29, N28 and O28, paying 15 each; the crown pays 5 each, as offered. She now has twenty-four sea squares.")
say("Paddy", "Four more fields at your five a square, Rich: sixty from me, twenty from the crown, all into the Pile in front of Clark's book. Thank you for the Wall, honestly, even if it's the north's roof and not mine. I'm building down the far end, away from wherever Rob's soldier fancies a paddle next.",
 "Paddy: That's eleven squares now, all of them well south of W2 at R25, and I've still got a hundred and nine grain left. That's a big purse, like Rob asked for, and I mean to put it in front of him for that Customs House.")
buy_land(6, ["T29", "T30", "U30", "V30"], {6:60, 1:20}, "New land: Paddy buys four squares for Province 6, T29, T30, U30 and V30, paying 15 each; the crown pays 5 each, as offered. He now has eleven squares.")
act("No auctions: there's no stock, and nobody has sold a piece back.", map_hl=[])
march({"W2":"Q26", "W3":"J19"}, "Rob marches, raiding nothing (his disaster bars every raid this year): W1 stands on H16; W2 wades to Q26 in the sea, one square from Silke's Scroll on P25; W3 walks into empty Province 4 at J19.",
 hover="Rob's objective: one piece from each of Clark, Reina, Silke and Paddy. He has Reina's, Clark's and Paddy's; only Silke's is left.")
say("Rob", "My lieutenant has run off with my daughter, so this year nobody gets robbed but me. The lads are just going for a walk. Silke, it's a lovely bit of water by your Scroll, nothing more. And Paddy, I haven't forgotten your big purse. Bring it round at the end of the year and we'll have a cup of tea over T23, but I'm warning you, that Customs House has sentimental value now.",
 "Rob: With no raids allowed anywhere, I'm just moving pieces into place. W2 wades to Q26, one step from Silke's Scroll and the last piece I need, and W3 slips into empty Province 4 two steps from the Winter Palace, just as Rich finally starts spending on his Wall.")
salaries({2:30}, text="Salaries: Rich pays Clark 30, as Official and for reading the I Ching, as he promised. The crown ends the year with 48.")

# ===== CHAPTER 5: A Very Short Rental

# ============================== YEAR 9 ==============================
R(9, "Storms and raiders", "repairs")
act("Clark turns the year's I Ching card: Storms and Raiders. Every province collects a good harvest. Silke and Paddy each pay 2 to the Harvest Pile to repair their public works (Provinces 4 and 7 are empty); Rich, Clark, Reina and Rob each play their next personal disaster: the second for Rich, Clark and Reina, the third for Rob. The population holds at x2, 200 million.", map_hl=[2])
say("Clark", "Storms and Raiders. Every province gets a good harvest. Provinces 4, 5, 6 and 7 pay 2 million each to repair their works, which means the coast mends its own roads again. Provinces 1, 2 and 3 each play a Personal Disaster. That's the whole north, me included, so I can't call it favouritism this time. Population stays at x2, counter on 200 million, so read the x2 row. And Rich, the card says 'the Warlord's sense of humour.' I've written that down too, under the heading of things the crown still hasn't answered.",
 "Clark: A disaster in my own booklet costs me a few grain off a hundred and ninety-four, so the set stays loaded. The useful part is that Rich and Reina get hit as well, and a thin crown taking a disaster makes thirty a year harder to find.")
act("The card names Province 8 as well: Rob plays a personal disaster too, his third.", map_hl=[8])
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; the negative incomes are paid into it.")
act("Storms and Raiders: Silke and Paddy each pay 2 to the Harvest Pile to repair their public works.", deltas={pid(5):-2, pid(6):-2}, pile_delta=4, map_hl=[5,6])
disaster(1, "a fire in the palace kitchens takes the Emperor's banquet hall with it, and it must be rebuilt before the New Year.")
disaster(2, "a gale tears the roof off the Official's record office, and the clerks' wages go on drying out the ledgers.")
disaster(3, "bandits rob the Gentry's grain barge on the river.")
disaster(8, "a sandstorm buries half the Warlord's camp, and half his grain is lost in the dunes.")
say("Reina", "Good harvest everywhere, five hundred and ten to the silk coast and two-fifty to the rice, all paid out to the grain, and you can count it while I watch. Ten from each of you, no conditions, same as last year. I could ask for more in a year like this, and I'm not going to. And Silke, here's some free advice from your landlord: the desert's one Scroll short of a set, and W2 is standing on your doorstep. Keep that Scroll somewhere he can't reach it.",
 "Reina: Ten grain off seven hundred and fifty is nothing to Silke, and that's why I'm asking for so little. Everyone at this table can see which landlord didn't get greedy in a fat year. The real point was the Scroll warning: if Rob completes his set, every set on this board goes through his hands, so Silke keeping hers safe protects me too.")
act("Reina asks Silke and Paddy each for 10 rent, with no conditions. Above the minimum, rent is a negotiation: each owes 5 and may agree to more. (Reina has 76 grain; Silke has 751, Paddy 357.)", map_hl=[3,5,6])
rents(5, "Rents: Silke and Paddy each pay Reina the minimum, 5.")
say("Silke", "Ten, Reina, counted out loud like always: five you already have, and here's five more. A landlord who warns me about the desert when she could have asked for twenty has earned it. And yes, I've seen W2 on Q26 too. That Scroll isn't going to stand there and wait for him.",
 "Silke: Five grain out of seven hundred and forty-six buys me a Gentry who's spent three years being paid fairly and knows exactly who paid her. Now the real business: W2 is one round from P25, Rob's sitting on seventeen and a half grain after his sandstorm, and a broke Warlord might sell a Customs House cheap.")
act("Silke pays Reina 5 more, freely: 10 in all.", deltas={pid(5):-5, pid(3):5}, map_hl=[3,5], deal=True)
say("Paddy", "Here's your other five, Reina. Ten's what you asked for and ten's fair, and two-fifty off the rice is more than I ever thought I'd see from that province. You've kept the Pile honest, and I'd rather pay my landlord than owe her. Rob, I'm sorry about the sandstorm, truly. The big purse is still coming round to your tent at the end of the year, and maybe it'll sound better to you now.",
 "Paddy: Five grain is nothing against three hundred and fifty, and paying it keeps Reina friendly, which I'll need if I'm ever going to climb. Rob's down to seventeen and a half grain, so the Customs House on T23 may never be cheaper than it is this year, and I'll be watching to see if he's hungry enough to sell.")
act("Paddy pays Reina 5 more, freely: 10 in all.", deltas={pid(6):-5, pid(3):5}, map_hl=[3,6], deal=True)
say("Rich", "Thirty percent, my friends, the same as it's been for eight years. A good harvest doesn't give me licence to get greedy. The last two links of the Wall go up over Province 1, paid with real grain as I promised. In a year like this the crown pays ten a square on every new field on the coast, not five. And Clark, here's your thirty, not one grain short, so you can write that under whatever heading you like.",
 "Rich: Two hundred and forty in, and once the Wall, the land and Clark's thirty are paid, I'll finish the year with something like two hundred left. That's the first real cushion this crown has had, and it's there for the day I have to buy Rob a reason to look away from Silke's beach. Doubling the land money to ten costs me pennies next to Silke's five hundred, and it's much harder to call a revolt against a man who paid for your fields twice over.")
act("Rich declares a tax of 30%: 153 from Silke, 75 from Paddy and 6 each from Clark and Reina; Rob's income is 0. 240 in all. The crown builds the last two links of the Great Wall above Province 1, for 80. New land: the crown pays 10 of every square another province buys this year, and buys two squares for Province 1. At the year's end it pays Clark 30.", map_hl=[1])
taxes(.30, skip=(), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Thirty percent, two hundred and forty in all: Silke 153, Paddy 75, Reina 6, and my own 6 goes in first so nobody can say the collector skimmed himself. Rob, your income's nothing this year, so you owe nothing, and I've written down that you said you'd pay. Rich, I've written your thirty as a promise kept, an eighth year. Under the same heading I've written that the crown is putting up links three and four of the Wall over Province 1, which makes four of four over your own roof and still none of four over mine. I'm not complaining. I'm keeping count.",
 "Clark: Silke pays a hundred and fifty-three this year while the crown's whole Wall goes over the Emperor's own province, so the coast is paying for the north's roof again and my strip is still shut. That's another line in the book, and Silke's the one I want reading it with me.")
build("Great Wall", 80, {1:80}, wall={1:2}, text="Public works: Rich builds the last two links of the Great Wall above Province 1, at the crown's cost of 80. All four links now stand over Province 1, and its strip below the Wall opens for new land.", hover="Rich pays the 80, into the Harvest Pile. 510 of the works are still owed.")
say("Silke", "Ten fields, Rich, at your ten a square: that's a hundred from the crown and a hundred from me. I paid a hundred and fifty-three in tax, so the crown is giving me back two-thirds of my own tax in mud. And Rob, here's twenty for one of your soldiers. You keep five, and he stands guard on my Scroll. Nothing personal. I just know where W2 is standing.",
 "Silke: Thirty-four fields by next year, all on the western side, nowhere near the beach where W2 is paddling. And the first soldier this coast has ever bought is standing on P25, so if Rob wants my Scroll for his set, he has to fight his own man for it.")
buy_land(5, ["H25", "H26", "H27", "H28", "H29", "I29", "J29", "J30", "K30", "L29"], {5:100, 1:100}, "New land: Silke buys ten squares of sea for Province 5, H25-H29, I29, J29, J30, K30 and L29, paying 10 each; the crown pays 10 each, as offered. She now has thirty-four sea squares.")
sell_soldiers([(5, "P25")], "Silke buys a soldier from Rob for 20, who must sell; he keeps 5 and 15 goes to the Harvest Pile. It stands on P25, on her Land Deed Scroll, and guards it: a raider must capture it first, straight on, and one raider can't break a free guard. Province 5 has been raided, so the soldier may move.")
say("Paddy", "Ten and ten on every square. Thank you, Rich, I'll take it gladly. Six more fields, all down the far end, as far from Q26 as the sea allows. I'll count out sixty, and Clark can write down that the rice paid its half before the crown paid its own.",
 "Paddy: That brings me to seventeen squares and still leaves me over two hundred grain, which is the big purse I promised to take round to Rob's tent. A man on seventeen and a half grain after a sandstorm might finally put a price on that Customs House.")
buy_land(6, ["S29", "S30", "T27", "T28", "V26", "V27"], {6:60, 1:60}, "New land: Paddy buys six squares for Province 6, S29, S30, T27, T28, V26 and V27, paying 10 each; the crown pays 10 each, as offered. He now has seventeen squares.")
act("Rich asked for F22 and G23 for Province 1, but both are already Province 1's own squares. Its new land is the sea touching the province or its bought squares, and, now that all four Wall links stand, the strip below the Wall.", map_hl=[1])
say("Rich", "Three fields for Province 1, sixty grain into Reina's Pile, and every grain of it counted. Clark, you can write that the Emperor finally farms a little of his own country. The Wall's done, and now the land behind it has to start paying its way.",
 "Rich: I've kept all three out on the north-eastern edge, as far from Rob's soldiers as my map goes, and next year they'll add sixty to every harvest. That still leaves the crown more than two hundred, which is plenty for Clark's thirty, the coast's ten a square and one quiet conversation with the desert.")
buy_land(1, ["D24", "E25", "F26"], {1:60}, "New land: Rich buys D24, E25 and F26 in the sea for Province 1, the crown paying the 60. Province 1 now has seven sea squares.")
act("No auctions: there's no stock, and nobody has sold a piece back.", map_hl=[])
march({"W1":"I17", "W2":"P26", "W3":"K20"}, "Rob marches: W1 crosses the north river onto I17 in Rich's Province 1, raiding 10 from the crown (and paying Rich the 5 toll); W2 steps ashore onto P26 in Silke's Province 5, raiding 5 from Silke, and stands straight beside her guard on the Scroll at P25; W3 walks on through empty Province 4 to K20.",
 hover="Rob's objective: one piece from each of Clark, Reina, Silke and Paddy. He has Reina's, Clark's and Paddy's; only Silke's is left, and it is guarded.")
say("Rob", "Silke, twenty grain for a soldier, and I didn't even have to ask. That's the nicest robbery I've ever been on the wrong end of. W2 is only popping round to the front door to say hello, five grain, very neighbourly. And Rich, half my grain went in a sandstorm, so I'm afraid my lads have to eat somewhere, and your garden's the closest kitchen. Paddy, the kettle's still on for the end of the year. Bring the purse and we'll talk about the weather.",
 "Rob: One guard can stop one soldier, so W3's started the long walk to the coast to make it two. Meanwhile W1 takes a nibble out of Rich's last fifty-eight and drifts toward the Winter Palace, just in case anyone at this table feels like pushing.")
troops({"Silke1":"P26"}, "The provinces' soldiers move: Silke's soldier steps off the Scroll, straight onto W2 on P26, and captures it. W2 leaves the board; Rob's army is down to two. The Scroll on P25 stands unguarded until Silke's soldier steps back.")
say("Silke", "Rob, you sold me that soldier for twenty, and you just marched your own man up to my front door to say hello. Here's my hello back. W2 comes off the board, and the five he raided is the last five he'll take off my coast. Nothing personal. You told me yourself it was the nicest robbery you'd ever been on the wrong end of.",
 "Silke: The only soldier who was ever one round from my Scroll is gone, W3 is five rounds off and W1 is eight, and my guard can step back onto P25 next year. Rob needed my Scroll to finish his set, and now he has to find another way there.")
salaries({2:30}, text="Salaries: Rich pays Clark 30, as Official and for reading the I Ching, as he promised. The crown ends the year with 23.")

# ============================== YEAR 10 ==============================
R(10, "Spring floods", "rainflood")
act("Clark turns the year's I Ching card: Spring Floods. Provinces 1, 3, 6 and 7 collect a good harvest; Provinces 2, 4, 5 and 8 a bad one. So Rich, Reina and Paddy have a good year, and Clark, Silke and Rob a bad one. The population holds at x2, 200 million.", map_hl=[2])
say("Clark", "Spring Floods. Provinces 1, 3, 6 and 7 get a good harvest. Provinces 2, 4, 5 and 8 get a bad one. Population stays at x2, counter on 200 million, so read the x2 row. That means the river drowns my fields and Silke's, and waters the Emperor's three brand-new ones nicely. Rich, Heaven seems to keep the same book you do. And the crown has twenty-three in it, against thirty owed to me at the end of the year. I mention that only so the number's on the record.",
 "Clark: Silke's soldier took W2 off the board, so Rob can't finish his set and I'm the only loaded gun at the table again, at full price. The crown has twenty-three and owes me thirty, so this is the year I find out whether that promise holds.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; the negative incomes are paid into it.")
say("Reina", "Paid out to the grain again: six-sixty to the silk coast, three-seventy to the rice, a hundred and forty to the crown's lucky new fields, and Clark's thirty and Rob's twenty come back into the Pile, so count it with me. Ten from each of you, no conditions, same as the last three years. And Silke, that was well played. Now get your man back onto that Scroll before anyone else comes round to say hello.",
 "Reina: Look at the numbers I just counted. The crown has twenty-three and owes Clark thirty, Clark's fields have flooded, and Rob is down to almost nothing with two soldiers left, so the north is the poorest it's been all game. I keep the rent at ten and the coast keeps thinking well of me, because when one of those men makes his move I want to be the one person here nobody holds a grudge against.")
act("Reina asks Silke and Paddy each for 10 rent, with no conditions. Above the minimum, rent is a negotiation: each owes 5 and may agree to more. (Reina has 110 grain; Silke has 1123, Paddy 582.)", map_hl=[3,5,6])
rents(5, "Rents: Silke and Paddy each pay Reina the minimum, 5.")
say("Silke", "Ten, Reina, the five you have and five more, counted out loud. A bad year on my coast still pays six hundred and sixty, so I won't plead poverty to a landlord whose Pile is paying it. And Rich, twenty-three in the crown against thirty owed to Clark is a number I'll be watching when you name the tax.",
 "Silke: Five grain keeps the one honest purse in the north on my side of the ledger. The real fight this year is the tax, because the crown is nearly empty, and I've got eleven hundred grain that Rich will want a share of.")
act("Silke pays Reina 5 more, freely: 10 in all. (The crown ended Year 9 with 23; after this year's harvest of +140 it holds 163.)", deltas={pid(5):-5, pid(3):5}, map_hl=[3,5], deal=True)
say("Paddy", "Ten, Reina, here's the other five. Three-seventy off the rice in a flood year is more than fair, and I'd not short you on it. Rob, I saw W2 go off the board, and I'm sorry for your trouble, truly. The purse is bigger than it was last year, and I'll still bring it round at the end of the year like I said.",
 "Paddy: Rob's down to two soldiers and has nothing left in the Pile, so this year he needs grain more than he needs a Customs House. With five hundred and seventy in my purse, I mean to make him a fair offer he can't laugh off.")
act("Paddy pays Reina 5 more, freely: 10 in all.", deltas={pid(6):-5, pid(3):5}, map_hl=[3,6], deal=True)
say("Rich", "Thirty percent, my friends, nine years running, flood or no flood. Clark, you've kept a very patient count, so this year the crown puts the first two links of the Wall over your roof and pays every grain itself. The coast gets five a square again, since ten was last year's gift for a good harvest. And yes, Clark, your thirty is there at the year's end, not one grain short.",
 "Rich: Last year's generosity cost me a hundred and sixty, which is a lesson in how fast Silke can buy mud, so the land money goes back to five. And the cheapest way I know to close Clark's book on me is eighty grain of Wall over his own head.")
act("Rich declares a tax of 30%: 198 from Silke, 111 from Paddy and 6 from Reina; Clark's and Rob's incomes are negative this year, so they owe nothing. 315 in all. The crown builds the first two links of the Great Wall above Province 2, Clark's, for 80, paying all of it. New land: the crown pays 5 of every square another province buys this year. At the year's end it pays Clark 30.", map_hl=[1,2])
taxes(.30, skip=(), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Thirty percent, three hundred and fifteen in all: Silke 198, Paddy 111, Reina 6. Rob and I lost grain to the river this year, so we owe nothing, and I've noted that Rob still says he pays. Rich, I'm writing this one down in full: two links of Wall over Province 2, paid by the crown, in Year Ten. That line has been empty since Year Seven, and now it's half full. Two links to go, and I'll be keeping count of those as well.",
 "Clark: Rich finally built over my roof, so I've written it down honestly, and a man who pays up is worth more to me on the throne than off it. For now. Rob is still one Scroll short of his set, and that's the line I'm watching closest.")
build("Great Wall", 80, {1:80}, wall={2:2}, text="Public works: Rich builds the first two links of the Great Wall above Province 2, Clark's, at the crown's whole cost of 80.", hover="Rich pays the 80, into the Harvest Pile. Two more links above Province 2 open Clark's strip for new land. 430 of the works are still owed.")
say("Silke", "Ten more fields, Rich, at your five a square. That's fifty from the crown and a hundred and fifty from me, and that's on top of a hundred and ninety-eight in tax. So count it properly, Clark: this year the coast put three hundred and forty-eight into the crown and the Pile, and got fifty back. Write that under whatever heading you keep for the people who pay for the Wall.",
 "Silke: Forty-four fields by next year, and every one of them pays for itself in a single harvest, which is more than you can say for any palace. Next I step my soldier back onto P25, because the next thing Rob takes off this coast won't be my Scroll.")
buy_land(5, ["G27", "G28", "G29", "G30", "H30", "I30", "L30", "M29", "N29", "O29"], {5:150, 1:50}, "New land: Silke buys ten squares of sea for Province 5, G27-G30, H30, I30, L30, M29, N29 and O29, paying 15 each; the crown pays 5 each, as offered. She now has forty-four sea squares.")
say("Paddy", "Fifteen and five is fair, Rich, and I'll not argue with it. Four more fields, every one of them down the far end beside the ones I bought last year. Sixty from me, and Clark can count it. And well done on the Wall over Clark's roof. I'd say that's the most patient count I've ever seen kept at a table.",
 "Paddy: Only four this year, because we're near the end of the game and I want the rest of my four hundred in my hand when I sit down for Rob's tea. A Customs House this late is worth more to me than any number of fields.")
buy_land(6, ["S28", "R29", "R30", "V25"], {6:60, 1:20}, "New land: Paddy buys four squares for Province 6, S28, R29, R30 and V25, paying 15 each; the crown pays 5 each, as offered. He now has twenty-one squares.")
say("Rich", "Four more fields up in the north-east corner, eighty grain into the Pile, counted. And Rob, you told us your lads have to eat somewhere. I'll buy one of them at your price, twenty, and you keep the five, and he can tell W1 dinner's over in my garden. Nothing personal, as Silke says. I'm a customer, and a very polite one.",
 "Rich: Rob taught me last year that every threat has a price, and this one's twenty grain, five of which go straight back to him, so he'll grumble but he can hardly call it unfair. Even after this the crown keeps nearly three hundred for the coast's five a square and Clark's thirty, so this is the richest I've been since Year 2.")
buy_land(1, ["C24", "D25", "D26", "E26"], {1:80}, "New land: Rich buys C24, D25, D26 and E26 in the sea for Province 1, the crown paying the 80. Province 1 now has eleven sea squares.")
sell_soldiers([(1, "H17")], "Rich buys a soldier from Rob for 20, who must sell; he keeps 5 and 15 goes to the Harvest Pile. It stands on H17 in Province 1, straight beside W1 on I17. Province 1 has been raided, so it may move, but the Warlord moves first: the provinces' soldiers move after his march.")
act("No auctions: there's no stock, and nobody has sold a piece back.", map_hl=[])
march({"W1":"H17", "W3":"L21"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps straight onto Rich's soldier on H17 and captures it, raiding the square for 10 from the crown; W3 walks on through empty Province 4 to L21.",
 hover="Rob's objective: one piece from each of Clark, Reina, Silke and Paddy. He has Reina's, Clark's and Paddy's; only Silke's is left.")
hire_soldier("T24", "Rob hires a new soldier, W2, for 20, paid to the Harvest Pile, on T24 in his southern desert beside the Customs House on T23, four rounds from Silke's Scroll. His army is back to three; the new soldier moves from next year.")
say("Rob", "Rich, I sold you that lad at a fair price, and you put him right next to W1, so W1 has gone over to shake his hand. Ten for the garden, and I'll call the twenty a very short rental. Silke, I learned that one from you, so thank you for the lesson. Paddy, the Customs House is still in the tent and the kettle's still warm. Bring the purse round, I always like hearing it.",
 "Rob: Rich's soldier was standing straight beside W1, so I captured him and took the crown's ten. Then I spend the money on a new man down south, so he and W3 can come at Silke's Scroll together. It's two raiders against one guard now, which is how it should have been done last year.")
troops({"Silke1":"P25"}, "The provinces' soldiers move: Silke's soldier steps back onto P25 and guards her Scroll again. W3 on L21 and the new W2 on T24 are each four rounds from it.")
say("Silke", "Back on the Scroll he goes. Rob, your new man on T24 is four rounds out and so is W3, and one raider can't break a free guard, so you'll need to bring both of them, and I'll see them coming. Rich, the lesson wasn't buying a soldier. The lesson was not parking him in front of the Warlord.",
 "Silke: One guard on P25 means Rob has to march two soldiers four rounds to take my Scroll, and that's four years of warning. I'll spend those years buying the Customs House out of that tent before Paddy's purse gets there.")
salaries({2:30}, text="Salaries: Rich pays Clark 30, as Official and for reading the I Ching, as he promised. The crown ends the year with 188.")

# ===== CHAPTER 6: I Never Sold You the House
# ============================== YEAR 11 ==============================
R(11, "Fat years make large families", "double3")
act("Before the card is turned, the table agrees to stop after Year 12 and score from where everyone sits then: a seat with a lower number than the one you drew is a win, the same seat a hold, a higher one a loss. The Emperor still on the throne and a Warlord who has entered the social order have won. Ties go by grain.", map_hl=[])
act("Clark turns the year's I Ching card: Population Doubles. Every province collects a good harvest, and the population doubles from x2 to x4, 400 million, so every province eats twice what it did.", map_hl=[2])
say("Clark", "Population Doubles. Fat years make large families. Every province gets a good harvest, and then every province eats twice what it ate before. The counter goes from two hundred million to four hundred million, so read the times-four row, all of you. Before anyone asks, I've checked the card against my own row and it's no kinder to me than to anyone else. My strip is still half walled and every new mouth in Province 2 is one I can't plough for. Rich, that's the second Wall line I'll be reading out this year, and I'll read it in the same even voice as the first.",
 "Clark: At times four my average year runs eighty-five short. The only cure is the last two Wall links over Province 2, and nobody can build them except the man who pays my thirty. That makes the throne's own spending the most interesting line in my book for the last two years, and I'm holding a loaded set while I read it.")
act("At x4 the desert's good harvest is 40 and Rob's army eats 80, so his income this year is -40, and he holds 7.5. Before a payment would put a player in debt, they must first sell realia back to the Harvest Pile at 2 a piece (Note 7). Before the harvest is paid, Rob offers Paddy the Customs House on T23, Paddy's own piece that W2 raided in Year 6, for 30.", map_hl=[6, 8])
say("Rob", "Paddy, my friend, you've brought that purse round every year, and tonight the tea's finally ready. Thirty grain and your Customs House goes home to Province 6. Or you can watch the Pile auction it off to whoever's got the most money, and we all know who's got the most money. Fat years, everybody. Lovely for families, terrible for a man feeding an army on sand.",
 "Rob: The raid stays on my record whatever happens to the piece, so the Customs House is just furniture now, and thirty from Paddy keeps me out of debt so nobody gets a free excuse to revolt against me. Silke's Scroll is still four steps from two of my soldiers, and that's the only piece I care about.")
say("Paddy", "Thirty grain, Rob, and that's a fair number. It's the first one you've named in four years, and I'd have paid it in Year 7. Here it is, every grain counted out, and I'll thank you for the tea. Clark, once the harvest's in I'd like a word with you about that Jar and Scroll, out in the open, the same way.",
 "Paddy: That's one of my three pieces back for thirty grain, out of a purse of four hundred, and I'm putting it at R20 where no soldier on the board can reach it this year or next. Clark's sitting on a spare Jar and Scroll, so he's the man I'll be watching now.")
give(pid(8), pid(6), "CH", "R20")
act("Paddy buys the Customs House from Rob for 30 and places it on R20 in Province 6.", deltas={pid(6):-30, pid(8):30}, map_hl=[6, 8], deal=True)
sell_back(pid(8), ["CH", "DJ"], "Rob holds 37.5 against the harvest's 40, so he sells his other two pieces back to the Harvest Pile at 2 each: the Customs House on N14 and the Dragon Jar on M15. With 41.5 he covers the harvest and isn't in debt. The Pile auctions both pieces at this year's auction.",
 hover="Rob's raids stay on his record: one piece from each of Reina, Clark and Paddy. Only Silke's is still to take.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; the negative incomes are paid into it.")
say("Reina", "The Pile pays out to the grain: Silke eight hundred and thirty, Paddy three hundred and seventy, Rich a hundred, and Clark, Rob and I pay in. Now the rent. For eight years I've asked the coast for five or ten, and in the bad years I took the floor. Tonight Silke's holding sixteen hundred and Paddy seven hundred and forty, and my own province eats sixty more than it grows, so I'm asking fifty from Silke and twenty-five from Paddy. No conditions, the same as always, and I'm not asking you to promise me anything at the auction. If either of you thinks I haven't earned it, pay me five and say so in front of everyone, and I'll take it with good grace.",
 "Reina: That's three percent of Silke's purse and I've never asked for more than ten, so it's the cheapest goodwill the coast will ever spend. And there's a Customs House and a Dragon Jar going back up for auction this year, so I want to walk in with more than sixty-four grain when Clark starts outbidding me by one.")
act("Reina asks Silke for 50 rent and Paddy for 25, with no conditions. Above the minimum, rent is a negotiation: each owes 5 and may agree to more. (Reina has 64 grain; Silke has 1595, Paddy 741.)", map_hl=[3,5,6])
rents(5, "Rents: Silke and Paddy each pay Reina the minimum, 5.")
say("Silke", "Fifty, Reina, and it's earned. Eight years you asked for the floor when you could have asked for more, and you never once put a string on it. There's the other forty-five, counted out: you're at a hundred and nineteen and I'm at fifteen hundred and forty-five, and nobody here will say the coast starved its Gentry. Rob, that Customs House in the Pile is coming to Province 5 tonight, so bring what you've got. It's one and a half.",
 "Silke: Forty-five grain is less than three percent of my purse, and it makes sure the Gentry seat I may be sitting in next year isn't taken over by someone with a grudge. Paddy's on seven hundred and thirty-six, so he's the only one who can push that Customs House up at the auction, and I want Reina wishing me well when he does.")
act("Silke pays Reina 45 more, freely: 50 in all.", deltas={pid(5):-45, pid(3):45}, map_hl=[3,5], deal=True)
say("Paddy", "Twenty-five it is, Reina, the whole of it. You've kept that Pile honest for eight years, and you've taken the floor from me in the lean ones when you could've asked for more. It wouldn't sit right with me to hold seven hundred and forty and pay you five.",
 "Paddy: Twenty grain is nothing to me this year, and Reina has always been straight with me, so I won't haggle with her. Clark's Jar and Scroll are part of his own set, so they're not for sale. That leaves Rob's old Jar coming up at the Pile's auction and Silke's Scroll on P25, and those are the two I'll be chasing.")
act("Paddy pays Reina 20 more, freely: 25 in all.", deltas={pid(6):-20, pid(3):20}, map_hl=[3,6], deal=True)
say("Rich", "Thirty percent, my friends, ten years running, through flood and fat years and families doubling. A crown that changes its word with the weather isn't worth bowing to. Clark, you can read out that second Wall line in your even voice, because tonight the last two links go up over Province 2, every grain of it the crown's, and your strip opens. Your thirty comes at the year's end as always, not one grain short. And the coast gets five a square, the same as last year. Silke, I've watched you buy mud like tea, so do try to leave me a little to build with.",
 "Rich: Three hundred and sixty in, a hundred and ten out plus the coast's fields. That leaves the jar over five hundred going into the last year, and on the last night grain in hand breaks the ties. I've closed off Clark's last grievance with the Wall and I've kept the rate where Silke can't call it greed, so nobody gets a reason to call a revolt before the table stops.")
act("Rich declares a tax of 30%: 249 from Silke and 111 from Paddy; Clark's, Reina's and Rob's incomes are negative this year, so they owe nothing. 360 in all. The crown builds the last two links of the Great Wall above Province 2, Clark's, for 80, paying all of it, so Province 2's strip opens for new land. New land: the crown pays 5 of every square another province buys this year. At the year's end it pays Clark 30.", map_hl=[1,2])
taxes(.30, skip=(), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Great Wall", 80, {1:80}, wall={2:2}, text="Public works: Rich builds the last two links of the Great Wall above Province 2, Clark's, at the crown's whole cost of 80. All four links now stand over Province 2, and its strip below the Wall opens for new land.", hover="Rich pays the 80, into the Harvest Pile. 350 of the works are still owed.")
say("Clark", "Silke, two hundred and forty-nine. Paddy, a hundred and eleven. Counted twice, three hundred and sixty to the crown, and nobody's touched a grain on the way. Rich, I said I'd read the second Wall line in an even voice, so here it is: four of four over Province 2, crown's cost, Year Eleven. The line's full, it's closed, and it's written in ink. Now, since the strip you've just opened is a strip you kept shut for ten years, I'll take three squares on the far side, F11, F12 and G11, and I'd call ten a square from the crown a fair way to settle the arrears. Paddy, my door's open after the auction, out in the open, as you said.",
 "Clark: A square bought now pays back once, and at five from the crown that's five grain of profit a square, at ten it's ten, so I'm haggling over the tie-break and not the farm. I've put them as far from W1 on H17 as the strip allows, and Paddy wanting a public word about my Jar and Scroll is the most interesting thing anyone's said to me all game.")
act("Clark asks for three squares of the newly opened strip, F11, F12 and G11, and asks the crown for 10 a square rather than the 5 it offered.", map_hl=[1,2])
say("Silke", "Ten more squares, Rich, at your five a square: a hundred and fifty from me, fifty from the crown, and that leaves you plenty to build with. Count the tax I just paid, though: two hundred and forty-nine. With your fifty that makes three hundred, so the coast is still paying for its own mud and your Wall too. Congratulations on the open strip, Clark. It took four links over your roof before the crown remembered there was a sea.",
 "Silke: Each square pays back twenty once, in Year 12, against fifteen out of my purse, so I come out ahead and the crown spends grain it can't use to outbid me. I've kept off Q26 and Q27, where W2 could walk straight in from T24.")
buy_land(5, ["F27", "F28", "F29", "F30", "M30", "N30", "O30", "P27", "P28", "P29"], {5:150, 1:50}, "New land: Silke buys ten squares of sea for Province 5, F27-F30, M30, N30, O30 and P27-P29, paying 15 each; the crown pays 5 each, as offered. She now has fifty-four sea squares.")
say("Paddy", "Four more fields down the far end, Rich, at your five a square, and thank you for keeping to it again. I'll leave Silke the rest of the mud, because I want my purse full for the auction tonight.",
 "Paddy: Each of these squares costs me fifteen and pays back about twenty from next year's harvest, so they're a small sure gain, and they're nowhere near Rob's man on T24. The real grain stays in my hand, because Rob's old Jar is coming up at the Pile's auction and I don't mean to lose it by a grain the way I did in Year 2.")
buy_land(6, ["Q28", "Q29", "Q30", "R28"], {6:60, 1:20}, "New land: Paddy buys four squares for Province 6, Q28, Q29, Q30 and R28, paying 15 each; the crown pays 5 each, as offered. He now has twenty-five squares.")
say("Rich", "Clark, my dear friend, you'll have your five a square the moment the ink's dry, the same five Silke had and the same five Paddy had. I said five for every province tonight, and a crown that pays one man double in front of the coast won't hear the end of it until the dynasty falls. Your strip is open, and that's the arrears settled: four links of Wall, every grain of it the crown's.",
 "Rich: Fifteen grain is nothing to the jar, but if Clark gets ten while Silke gets five, Silke has her grievance with a year still to play, and I'm not handing her that. One price for everybody is a promise nobody can call unfair, so I'm sticking to it.")
buy_land(2, ["F11", "F12", "G11"], {2:45, 1:15}, "New land: Rich holds the crown's share at 5 a square, so Clark buys F11, F12 and G11 on Province 2's strip, paying 15 each; the crown pays 5 each. Province 2's first new land.")
act("Rich buys no new squares for Province 1 this year.", map_hl=[1])
act("The auction: the Harvest Pile sells the two pieces Rob sold back, the Customs House and then the Dragon Jar. The Pile keeps the whole price. Rob, with 1.5, can't cover the minimum of 2.", map_hl=[])
say("Clark", "Five a square, the same as the coast. I've written it down as fair, Rich, and I've also written down that I asked. I don't need either of these lots and I won't pretend I do. But I've spent a good deal of this game buying pieces I didn't need, and I'm not going to stop now. Paddy, the Jar's yours to fight for, and I won't stand in your way. Reina, I'm sorry to say I've still got a pencil behind my ear.",
 "Clark: Reina needs both of these lots, and if she gets them I'm not the only loaded gun at the table any more with a single year to go. Between them my keeps leave me at least twenty-three even if I win both, so the set stays loaded whatever happens.")
say("Reina", "Silke, in Year 4 I told you that if we wanted the same piece we'd talk it over before the auction, so here's the talk: the Customs House is yours, and I won't bid a grain against you. All I ask in return is that you remember who stood aside when you're sitting at the next set. The Jar's another matter. Paddy, I'll bid you for it honestly, and whatever it goes for, three quarters goes into the Pile I keep.",
 "Reina: Silke has eleven hundred grain, so fighting her for that Customs House would only have made me poorer and turned the coast against me, and keeping my Year 4 promise in public is the one thing it can buy. I'll go to eighty on the Jar and no further, because a set still needs twenty in hand, and Clark is sitting on both Bowls.")
say("Silke", "That Customs House has been lying around this table for eleven years, and tonight it comes to the coast. Anyone who wants to bid it up is welcome, but three quarters of every grain goes into the Pile and the crown's works, so count what you're throwing away first. Paddy, you can have your Jar, but if you mean to climb, you'll climb past my Scroll, so don't plan on it coming cheap.",
 "Silke: Reina has a hundred and thirty-nine, so Paddy is the only one who can make that Customs House expensive, and I've got the grain to see him off whatever he does. I'll let the Jar go to Paddy at a fair price, but not for nothing, because a Rice Peasant with a full set is looking straight at my seat.")
say("Paddy", "I've got my Customs House, thanks to Rob, so the first lot's not mine to fight over and I'll sit on my hands for it. The Jar's a different matter. I've lost lots by a grain before, and I won't lose this one that way. Reina, no hard feelings whichever way it goes.",
 "Paddy: My limit on the Jar is two hundred. Reina has a hundred and thirty-nine, so that beats her, and it still leaves me plenty for a Scroll next year and the twenty I always keep back. Two pieces of three, and then Silke's Scroll on P25 is the last door I'll knock on.")
lot(PILE, "CH", [B(5,2)], at="P24", hover="Silke: That Customs House has been lying around this table for eleven years, and tonight it comes to the coast.")
act("The Dragon Jar comes out level: Paddy's limit and Silke's (bid only to keep it from him) are both 200, which goes to Silke on seat order. Each gets one raise.", map_hl=[5,6])
say("Paddy", "Silke, you don't need that Jar, and everybody here knows it. I do. I'll go one more step for it, and that's the last step, because I won't throw the whole rice harvest into the Pile over a pot. If you go past me, it's yours, and I'll say so plainly.",
 "Paddy: Silke's purse is twice mine, so if she really means to stop me she can, and I can't win a war with her. Three hundred tells her I'm serious, and it still leaves me two hundred and forty-five for a Scroll and my twenty, so I've a chance of finishing the set in Year 12 if she stays put.")
say("Silke", "Paddy, I mean no harm by it, but count the board with me: you have seven hundred and forty grain, a Customs House, and Clark's Scroll one handshake away. I don't sell my own ladder to the man standing beside me on it. If you outbid me, you'll have earned the Jar, and the Pile will have earned a good deal more.",
 "Silke: Paddy can't bid past five hundred and forty-five, so five hundred and fifty means the Jar can't reach him, and it leaves me the only peasant still climbing, with plenty left for Clark's spare Bowl. It's expensive, but three quarters of it goes into the Pile, not into a rival's set.")
act("Paddy has 545, not 740; and on a lot the Harvest Pile sells, the Pile keeps the whole price.", map_hl=[])
lot(PILE, "DJ", [B(3,80), B(3,None,"Take it with my blessing, then. I'd rather the coast hold it than the desert."), B(6,200), B(5,200), B(6,300), B(6,None,"Fair enough, it's yours, and I'll go and ask about it honestly some other way."), B(5,301)], at="O25",
    hover="Clark bid only to keep the Jar from Reina, up to 55. Paddy and Silke tied at 200 and each raised once: Paddy to 300, Silke to 550.")
say("Silke", "Thank you, Paddy, I'll take the correction gladly: five hundred and forty-five, and the Pile keeps all three hundred and one. That's the most grain this coast has ever put back into the common bank in one night, and I'll ask Reina to write it down. The Customs House goes on P24 and the Jar on O25, right beside my man on P25, so if anyone wants either of them, they come through him.",
 "Silke: All three of my pieces now sit together in the corner, with my soldier beside them and every Warlord soldier at least two rounds away, so I'm one Wooden Bowl short, and Clark has two. I've got eight hundred and forty-three left to pay for it, and Clark is going to hear an offer from me before Year 12's auction.")
march({"W1":"G18", "W3":"M22", "W2":"S25"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto G18 in Rich's Province 1, raiding 10 from the crown; W3 walks on through empty Province 4 to M22; W2 steps onto S25 in Paddy's Province 6, raiding 5 from Paddy. No hire.",
 hover="Rob's objective: one piece from each of Clark, Reina, Silke and Paddy. He has raided Reina's, Clark's and Paddy's; Silke's pieces on P24, P25 and O25 are four rounds from W2 and W3, with one march left.")
say("Rob", "Congratulations on the Wall, Rich. It's a beautiful thing, and I'm sure it'll stop anyone who comes from the north, which is why I'm already south of it. Ten from the garden please, and Paddy, five from the rice for the walk, nothing personal. Silke, you've parked all your treasures next to one man, so I'll take that as an invitation.",
 "Rob: Silke's pieces are four rounds out and there are only two marches left, so the collection's finished whatever I tell the table, and from here on it's about grain for the tie-break and looking dangerous. W1 is taking ten off the crown on his way toward the palaces, W2 is taking five off Paddy, and W3 keeps walking at Silke so she has to watch the desert and not her neighbours.")
act("The provinces' soldiers move: Silke's soldier stays on P25, on her Scroll.", map_hl=[5])
say("Silke", "Rob, an invitation needs a date on it, and by my count W2 and W3 are both four rounds from my door, with the table stopping in one. Have your tea in Paddy's rice. My man stays where he's stood all along.",
 "Silke: Every Warlord soldier is four rounds from all three of my pieces, and only one year is left, so moving would only open a gap. The guard stays on the Scroll, and I put my mind to Clark's spare Bowl.")
salaries({2:30}, text="Salaries: Rich pays Clark 30, as Official and for reading the I Ching, as he promised, a tenth year. The crown ends the year with 443.")

# ============================== YEAR 12 ==============================
R(12, "The seed rotted", "dribble2")
act("Clark turns the year's I Ching card: Bad Harvest. Every province collects a bad harvest. The population holds at x4, 400 million.", map_hl=[2])
say("Clark", "Bad Harvest. The seed rotted, the rains came late, and the ox went lame. Every province gets a bad harvest. The population stays at four hundred million, so you're all still on the times-four row, and I'd read it twice tonight. Rich, ten years of thirty, paid to the grain, and I've written every one of them down. This is the first lean year since the treasury filled, which is exactly what I said I'd be watching. It's also the last year, and in my book the last page is where the totals go.",
 "Clark: I'm the only complete, loaded set at the table, the throne is Province 1, and I drew Province 2, so this is the year my patience either gets paid or gets spent. Silke needs one Bowl, I've got a spare one on G15, and that's leverage with a very short shelf life.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; the negative incomes are paid into it.")
act("Rob had 16.5 and no pieces left to sell back, so the harvest puts him 43.5 in debt to the Harvest Pile. In debt he cannot bid, buy, trade, give grain, hire or change seats, and every grain he receives pays the debt first. A player in debt is a cause for revolt.", map_hl=[8])
say("Reina", "The Pile pays out to the grain: Silke nine hundred and eighty, Paddy four hundred, Rich fifty. Clark, Rob and I pay in, and Rob's forty-three and a half goes on the books as a debt, written in ink. And Silke, I've written yours down too: three hundred and one, the most the coast has ever put into the common bank. For the rent I'm asking the same as last year, fifty from Silke and twenty-five from Paddy, with no conditions. I won't raise it on the last night just because I could.",
 "Reina: Bad harvest at times four took a hundred and ten off me, so this rent is the difference between ending the game in the black and ending it as the Gentry who couldn't pay her own Pile. And now a debtor's sitting on the board and Silke is one Bowl short of a set, with Clark holding two of them, so whoever moves on the throne tonight, I'd like to be standing beside the coast when they do.")
act("Reina asks Silke for 50 rent and Paddy for 25 again, with no conditions. Each owes 5 and may agree to more. (Reina has 29 grain; Silke has 1823, Paddy 940.)", map_hl=[3,5,6])
rents(5, "Rents: Silke and Paddy each pay Reina the minimum, 5.")
say("Silke", "Fifty again, Reina, every grain of it. You stood aside for me on the Customs House, and I said I'd remember who did. Clark, you've got two Bowls and a hundred and three grain, and your book needs a total on the last page, so here's a big one: three hundred for the spare on G15, counted out in front of everyone, tonight.",
 "Silke: Three hundred nearly quadruples Clark's purse, and he only needs one Bowl for his own set, so he has every reason to say yes and only one to say no: which seat I sit in after. I haven't promised him anything about that, and I haven't promised Reina either.")
act("Silke pays Reina 45 more, freely: 50 in all. She offers Clark 300 for his spare Wooden Bowl on G15, the last piece her set needs.", deltas={pid(5):-45, pid(3):45}, map_hl=[2,3,5], deal=True)
say("Paddy", "The same as last year, Reina, twenty-five in all, and gladly. You kept the rate where it was on the last night when you could've asked for more, and that's worth something to me. Silke, the Jar went fairly, and I said I'd take it with no hard feelings. I meant it.",
 "Paddy: The set's gone for this game, because Silke's got the only spare Jar and she's sitting right beside it. So I'll finish the way I started, in my own seat, with every grain I owe paid and nobody able to say Paddy Doyle shorted them.")
act("Paddy pays Reina 20 more, freely: 25 in all.", deltas={pid(6):-20, pid(3):20}, map_hl=[3,6], deal=True)
say("Clark", "Silke, that's the handsomest figure anyone's put to me in twelve years, and I'm writing it on the last page with a box round it. But read the rule with me: with that Bowl in hand you could sit in any seat but the throne, mine included, and the man in your seat walks off with my hundred and three and your three hundred on top. Nobody has ever paid me three hundred to stand under my own guillotine, and you're a friend, so I won't make you try. The Bowl stays on G15. My loyalty was rented by the year, and the lease on this one isn't up for sale.",
 "Clark: If I sell that Bowl I'm handing her the only key to my own chair on the very last night, and no sum of grain is worth falling from seat two to seat five. Keeping it means I'm still the only loaded set at the table, and Rich has just heard me say so.")
act("Clark refuses Silke's 300 for the Bowl. With it she could have changed seats with him; the Bowl stays on G15.", map_hl=[2,5])
say("Rich", "Thirty percent, my friends, eleven years running, through a bad harvest and on the last night, when I could have asked for anything I liked. Silke, the second section of your canal goes in tonight, every grain of it the crown's, because the coast has carried this treasury and I won't let anyone write that I forgot it. Clark, your thirty comes at the year's end, an eleventh time and not one grain short, so put it in the box on your last page. And five a square for anyone still buying mud.",
 "Rich: Same rate, a canal for the woman most likely to call a revolt, and the Official paid to the letter, so there's no grievance on the table on the last night. The jar finishes well over eight hundred, and the throne stays where it's been since Year 2.")
act("Rich declares a tax of 30%: 294 from Silke and 120 from Paddy; Clark's, Reina's and Rob's incomes are negative, so they owe nothing. 414 in all. The crown digs the second section of the Grand Canal in Province 5, Silke's, for 40. New land: the crown pays 5 of every square another province buys. At the year's end it pays Clark 30.", map_hl=[1,5])
taxes(.30, skip=(), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={5:1}, text="Public works: Rich digs the second section of the Grand Canal in Province 5, Silke's, at the crown's cost of 40. Both sections of Province 5's canal now stand.", hover="Rich pays the 40, into the Harvest Pile. 310 of the works are still owed.")
say("Clark", "Silke, two hundred and ninety-four. Paddy, a hundred and twenty. Counted twice, four hundred and fourteen to the crown, and not one grain skimmed. That's the last tax I'll collect, and it went in honest. Rich, I promised you a total on the last page, so here it is. Eleven years of thirty, every one paid, and every one credited in ink. Seven years before a single link of Wall over my roof. A Warlord who's taken three provinces' grain, walked off with my Jar and now sits forty-three and a half in debt to the Pile, which this book lists as a cause for revolt, and not one soldier ever sent after him. You paid the rent beautifully, my friend, but I never sold you the house. The set's on the board and the grain's in my hand: I'm taking the throne.",
 "Clark: The deal was always rented by the year, and this is the one year where nobody comes back to pay next year's rent. I drew seat two and the throne is seat one, so tonight the paper trail turns into a coronation.")
swap(2, 1, "Clark, with his complete set (the Land Deed Scroll, Dragon Jar and Wooden Bowl) and 103 grain in hand, replaces the Emperor. Clark takes the throne and the crown's reserve; every piece he held goes back to its province's stock to be auctioned: the Scroll to Province 3, the Jar to Province 5, both Bowls to Province 6. Rich becomes the Official in Province 2, holds nothing, and draws 60 from the Harvest Pile.",
 hover="Clark drew the Official's seat, Rich the Gentry's. Seat changes may be made at any step; Rich took the throne at the rent step in Year 2.")
say("Silke", "No mud tonight, Rich. A field that never brings in a harvest is a palace I'll never sit in, and thank you for the canal, it came late but it came. Clark, you've told me what you're afraid of, so here's the answer in front of witnesses: five hundred for the spare Bowl, and I swear on my fifty-four fields I won't sit in your chair. Write that in the box too, and you end the game in your own seat with six hundred and three grain.",
 "Silke: Clark's only reason to refuse was his own neck, so I've taken it off the table and quintupled his purse. If he still says no, he's guarding the Gentry's seat for Reina, not his own, and everyone at this table can count what that means.")
act("Silke buys no new land. Her 500 for the Bowl comes too late: Clark has already taken the throne, and both Bowls are back in Province 6's stock, so Paddy must auction one this year.", map_hl=[5,6])
say("Paddy", "No mud for me tonight, Rich, and thank you for the five all the same. A field I put in the sea tonight would never grow a single grain before the table stops, and I won't pretend otherwise. Clark, I think you're right to keep that Bowl. I'd have done the same in your chair.",
 "Paddy: There's no harvest after this one, so any field I buy now is grain thrown away, and my purse is all that breaks a tie. I'll keep my seven hundred and ninety-five and finish where I started, in Province 6 and owing nobody.")
act("Paddy buys no new land.", map_hl=[6])
act("The auction: Clark's old pieces go up from their provinces' stock, Reina's Land Deed Scroll, Silke's Dragon Jar and one of Paddy's two Wooden Bowls. Clark, as Emperor, doesn't bid, and Rob, in debt, can't.", map_hl=[3,5,6])
say("Reina", "Well played, Clark. Eleven years of ink, and the last page turns out to be a deed. I'll put the Scroll up from Province 3 at ten, which is a fair floor for a piece that's just made an Emperor, and I won't be bidding on the Jar or the Bowls. I've no Customs House, and I'm not going to pour the Gentry's grain into a set I can't finish before the table stops. Silke, Paddy, whatever you do with those Bowls tonight, do it out in the open, the way this coast has done everything.",
 "Reina: I said in Year 4 that the man paid to guard the crown was the one best placed to take it, and it took him eight years to prove me right. With only one round left I can't build a set, so the most I can do is sell my Scroll at a decent price, keep my hundred and four, and end the game as the honest Gentry with the most grain I've had since I lost the chair.")
say("Silke", "Well played, Clark, and I mean it: you counted better than any of us, and I'll bow when the night's over. Paddy, I'm bidding on your Bowl, and anyone who wants to bid against me should know I'll put up every grain in my purse except the twenty the set needs, all of it going into the Pile. The Jar goes up at two, and whoever wins it gets it honestly.",
 "Silke: There's only one Bowl on the block, Clark is the only one rich enough to block me, and he can't afford to empty his purse to do it, so my ceiling is the whole purse minus the twenty grain. If I get it, I'll take the Official's seat Clark just left behind, which means Rich has gone from throne to Official to the coast in one night.")
say("Paddy", "Well, Clark, you've done what you said you'd do, and you did it in the open, so I can't fault you for it. That puts the Scroll and the Jar back on the table, and I'll bid on both of them plainly. I've wanted those two since Year 2, and tonight I've got the grain to mean it. The Bowl goes up at two, the same as anybody's. I won't make a man pay a toll because he's coming up behind me.",
 "Paddy: Three hundred and seventy-five on the Scroll and four hundred on the Jar, and even if I win both at my limit there's still twenty in my purse, so the set would be complete and Reina's seat in Province 3 would be in reach. Silke's the one who could stop me if she wants it badly enough, so I'm watching her hand.")
say("Rich", "Well played, Clark, well played. Eleven years of thirty, to the letter, and you paid me back with the chair. I'd have done it myself, and I did, in Year 2. Your Majesty will find the reserve fuller than I found it, and I'll collect your taxes as honestly as you collected mine. Meanwhile an old man with sixty grain will raise his hand once or twice tonight, just to keep the auctioneers honest.",
 "Rich: To take the throne back I need all three pieces and twenty grain left over, so I can spend forty at most, and Paddy and Silke both have hundreds. It's a long shot, but the only one I've got, and if Silke's Bowl puts her in Clark's chair instead, I won't shed a tear for my dear friend's guillotine.")
act("The Emperor's chair is Clark's now, so Silke's line about him blocking her misreads the table: the Emperor doesn't bid. And the Official's seat she names is Rich's.", map_hl=[])
lot(3, "LD", [B(2,12), B(2,None,"Take it, with my blessing. A man with sixty grain knows his limit."), B(6,13)], at="Q19", hover="Reina set the minimum at 10.")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(2,12), B(2,None,"Take it, with my blessing. A man with sixty grain knows his limit."), B(6,13)], at="Q20")
lot(6, "WB", [B(2,16), B(2,None,"Take it, with my blessing. A man with sixty grain knows his limit."), B(5,17)], at="P23", hover="Silke's ceiling was 1459.")
say("Paddy", "Thirteen and thirteen. After all these years, I can't quite believe it. Rich, you kept every word you ever gave a rice farmer, and I'm sorry it's your chair. But the crown's already gone from you tonight, and I won't knock Reina out of a seat she's kept honest for eleven years. The Customs House goes back to Province 2's stock with my thanks to Rob, and I'll collect Clark's taxes the way he collected ours: counted twice, and not one grain skimmed.",
 "Paddy: Province 2 is four seats up from where I drew, so that's a win, and I'm taking it from the one man at the table who's already lost his. Silke chooses after me, and since I've left Reina's seat alone I'd guess that's where she's headed.")
swap(6, 2, "Paddy, with his complete set (the Customs House, Land Deed Scroll and Dragon Jar) and 20 grain in hand, replaces the Official. Paddy becomes the Official in Province 2 with his grain and Rich's; the Customs House, Province 2's own, goes back to its stock, and his Scroll and Jar move with him to G13 and I13. Rich takes the Rice Peasant's seat in Province 6, holds nothing, and draws 60 from the Harvest Pile.",
 hover="Paddy drew the Rice Peasant's seat. Rich, who drew the Gentry's, began the night on the throne.", at={"LD": "G13", "DJ": "I13"})
say("Silke", "Paddy, count it with me, because it's the kindest sum on the board: you drew the Rice Peasant, so the Silk Peasant's seat is still a step up for you, with fifty-four sea fields, two canal sections and a soldier on the Scroll. Reina stood aside for me on the Customs House, so her seat stays hers, as I said I'd remember. I'll take the Official's book, Clark, and I'll count your taxes out loud, every grain, the same as I've counted everyone else's for twelve years.",
 "Silke: Taking the Official's chair puts the Silk Peasant in Province 2, three seats above where she started, while Paddy still finishes above the seat he drew and Reina keeps hers, so nobody at the table loses by my climb except the man who was already falling. The coast paid for this chair for twelve years, and tonight it finally sits in it.")
swap(5, 2, "Silke, with her complete set (the Land Deed Scroll, Customs House and Wooden Bowl) and 20 grain in hand, replaces the Official. Silke becomes the Official in Province 2 with her grain and Paddy's; the Customs House goes back to Province 2's stock, and her Scroll, Jar and Bowl move with her to G12, G14 and I12. Paddy takes the Silk Peasant's seat in Province 5, with its soldier on P25; his Scroll and Jar go back to their provinces' stock, and he draws 60 from the Harvest Pile.",
 hover="Silke drew the Silk Peasant's seat, Paddy the Rice Peasant's: both finish higher than they drew.", at={"LD": "G12", "DJ": "G14", "WB": "I12"})
march({"W1":"H19", "W2":"R24"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto H19 in Clark's Province 1, raiding 10 from the crown; W2 steps onto R24 in Rich's Province 6, raiding 5 from Rich; W3 stands on M22. Every grain pays his debt first. No hire.",
 hover="Rob's objective counts only raids on players in realia-holding seats; with the seats changed tonight, Reina's and Paddy's raids still count, and no piece is within reach.")
say("Rob", "Your Majesty! Three new seats in a single evening, and every one of them taken in the open. I've never been prouder of a table of honest people. Clark, I'll be stopping by the garden for ten on the way to congratulate you properly, and Rich, I'm sorry, old friend, but the rice is yours now, and so is the five.",
 "Rob: Rich has no soldiers to stop a raid, so I'm taking ten off the new crown and five off the new rice farmer to cut what I owe the Pile, and W3 stays put because crossing the river costs as much as it would bring in. The chair changed hands three times tonight and I got nowhere near any of it, so the villain ends the night in debt and still smiling, and nobody has ever sent a single soldier after me.")
act("The provinces' soldiers: no Warlord soldier is within reach of one, and the province has no pieces left to guard. Paddy's soldier (the Silk Peasant's) stays on P25.", map_hl=[5])
say("Clark", "Silke, the Official's salary is thirty, the same thirty Rich paid me for eleven years without missing a grain or a day. A crown that changes the rate the moment it changes hands isn't worth bowing to. I'm told a man once said that at this very table. Rich, I've written your eleven payments on the last page in my best hand. A book that won't credit a payment is no use to anyone, and nobody at this table paid more honestly than you. Rob, the garden's ten is in the book as well, under your debt.",
 "Clark: I drew seat two and I'm finishing on the throne, so it's a win, and keeping the old rent going for Silke costs me nothing I'll miss. The man who keeps the paper trail ends up wearing it as a crown.")
salaries({2:30}, text="Salaries: Clark, the new Emperor, pays Silke, the new Official, 30. The crown ends the game with 930.")
act("The table stops here, after Year 12's salaries, as agreed, and scores from where everyone sits. 310 of the works are still owed, so the game ends in neither survival nor collapse, and no revolt was called in twelve years. Clark Grafton, who drew the Official (2), finishes on the throne (1); Silke Weber, who drew the Silk Peasant (5), is the Official (2); Paddy Doyle, who drew the Rice Peasant (6), is the Silk Peasant (5); Reina Osorio, who drew the throne, is the Gentry (3); Richard Baron, who drew the Gentry (3), is the Rice Peasant (6); Robert Land is still the Warlord (8), in debt.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])

LAST_ROUND = 12   # the agreed stop (Note 19), set by the Director before Chapter 6: the table stops after Year 12 and scores from where everyone sits

RESULT = export(
 "The table stopped after Year 12, as agreed (Note 19), with 310 of the works unbuilt. Players are ranked by the seat they finished in against the one they drew, with grain breaking ties.",
 ["Clark drew the Official and finished on the throne. He completed a set in Year 2 and held it for ten years while Rich paid him thirty a year to leave it unused, wrote down every deferral and never called a revolt. In Year 12 he collected the tax, refused Silke's 500 for his spare Bowl, and took the throne with the reserve.",
  "Silke drew the Silk Peasant and finished as the Official, the richest player at the table with 2,328. She bought land every year from the doubling, 54 sea squares in all, guarded her Scroll with a bought soldier that captured one of Rob's, and paid 301 for a Jar to keep it from Paddy. When Clark's pieces came back to auction she won a Bowl at 17 and climbed over Paddy the same night.",
  "Paddy drew the Rice Peasant and finished as the Silk Peasant. He won nothing at auction for two years, bought land every year, and bought his Customs House back from Rob for 30 in Year 11. In the last auction he won the Scroll and the Jar at 13 each, took the Official's seat from Rich, and lost it to Silke minutes later, still a seat above the one he drew.",
  "Reina drew the throne, taxed half the first harvest and built the Summer Palace, and lost it to Rich's set in Year 2. As the Gentry she raised the rent from 5 to 50, kept her Year 4 promise to stand aside for Silke's Customs House, and never held another piece after Rob took hers in Year 3.",
  "Rich drew the Gentry and took the throne with a set in Year 2. He ruled for ten years at 30% tax, paid Clark thirty a year without missing a grain, ran the reserve to nothing in the lean years and never went into debt, and paid towards every square of peasant land. Clark took the throne from him in Year 12, and Paddy took the Official's seat, so he finished as the Rice Peasant.",
  "Rob drew the Warlord and finished there, in debt. He paid his tax by choice, took Reina's, Clark's and Paddy's pieces, and lost a soldier to Silke's guard on the last name he needed. At x4 his harvest sank him: he sold Paddy's Customs House back for 30 and the other two to the Pile at 2 each, and ended at -28.5."])
